+# Event Routing Specification
+
+**Version:** 0.1
+**Status:** Draft
+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
+
+---
+
+# Purpose
+
+The Event Routing Specification defines how repository events are distributed between producers and consumers.
+
+Event routing transports events.
+
+It SHALL NOT modify event content or event semantics.
+
+---
+
+# Design Principles
+
+- Routing is deterministic.
+- Events are immutable.
+- Producers are independent of consumers.
+- Delivery is observable.
+- Routing preserves chronology.
+
+---
+
+# Scope
+
+This specification applies to:
+
+- Event producers
+- Event consumers
+- Event channels
+- Event subscriptions
+- Event delivery
+- Event routing infrastructure
+
+---
+
+# Event Routing Model
+
+The Repository Ingestion Framework SHALL implement a publish-subscribe routing model.
+
+The routing layer SHALL:
+
+- Receive events from producers.
+- Determine interested consumers.
+- Deliver events.
+- Record routing activity.
+
+Routing SHALL remain transparent to producers.
+
+---
+
+# Event Producers
+
+Examples of producers include:
+
+- Artifact Intake Service
+- Structural Parser
+- Asset Extractor
+- Validation Engine
+- Repository Mapper
+- Governance Service
+- Publication Service
+
+Producers SHALL publish completed events only.
+
+---
+
+# Event Consumers
+
+Examples of consumers include:
+
+- Observability
+- Repository Indexer
+- Audit Service
+- Analytics
+- Monitoring
+- Notification Services
+- Automation Workflows
+
+Consumers SHALL NOT modify received events.
+
+---
+
+# Event Channels
+
+Events SHOULD be organized into logical channels.
+
+Examples include:
+
+- Intake
+- Parser
+- Assets
+- References
+- Validation
+- Queue
+- Governance
+- Publication
+- Operations
+
+Channels improve organization without changing event meaning.
+
+---
+
+# Subscriptions
+
+Consumers SHALL explicitly declare:
+
+- Subscribed channels
+- Accepted event types
+- Supported event versions
+
+Subscriptions SHOULD be validated during initialization.
+
+---
+
+# Delivery Guarantees
+
+The routing system SHOULD provide:
+
+- Reliable delivery
+- Ordered delivery where applicable
+- Duplicate detection
+- Delivery confirmation
+- Failure reporting
+
+Delivery guarantees SHALL be documented by the implementation.
+
+---
+
+# Routing Failures
+
+Routing failures SHALL:
+
+- Preserve the original event.
+- Record diagnostics.
+- Support retry.
+- Avoid silent event loss.
+
+Failed delivery SHALL generate an operational event.
+
+---
+
+# Event Replay
+
+The routing system MAY support replay of historical events.
+
+Replay SHALL:
+
+- Preserve event order where possible.
+- Preserve original timestamps.
+- Clearly identify replay activity.
+
+Replay SHALL NOT alter event history.
+
+---
+
+# Event Filtering
+
+Consumers MAY filter events using:
+
+- Event Category
+- Event Type
+- Source Component
+- Subject Identifier
+- Severity
+- Correlation ID
+
+Filtering SHALL NOT modify event contents.
+
+---
+
+# Monitoring
+
+Routing infrastructure SHOULD report:
+
+- Published events
+- Delivered events
+- Failed deliveries
+- Retry attempts
+- Queue depth
+- Delivery latency
+
+Operational metrics SHALL support troubleshooting.
+
+---
+
+# Validation
+
+Routing SHALL verify:
+
+- Event schema validity.
+- Supported event version.
+- Valid destination.
+- Subscription compatibility.
+
+Invalid routing requests SHALL be rejected.
+
+---
+
+# Repository Integration
+
+Event routing integrates with:
+
+- Event Model
+- Event Catalog
+- Event Schema
+- Observability
+- Security
+- Audit
+- Repository Indexing
+
+Routing SHALL preserve repository integrity.
+
+---
+
+# Design Philosophy
+
+Events describe what has happened.
+
+Routing determines who needs to know.
+
+By separating event production from event consumption, the Repository Ingestion Framework remains modular, extensible, and capable of evolving without disrupting institutional knowledge.
+
+---
+
+End of Specification.
