+# Contents
+````markdown
+# Implementation Backlog
+
+**Version:** 0.1  
+**Status:** Draft  
+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
+
+---
+
+# Purpose
+
+The Implementation Backlog defines the canonical work items required to implement the Repository Ingestion Framework.
+
+Unlike specifications, backlog items represent executable engineering work.
+
+Backlog items evolve as implementation progresses.
+
+---
+
+# Design Principles
+
+- One responsibility per work item.
+- Work items should be independently testable.
+- Dependencies should be explicit.
+- Completion produces measurable capability.
+- Progress is incremental.
+
+---
+
+# Work Item Status
+
+Each work item SHALL exist in one of the following states:
+
+- Proposed
+- Planned
+- In Progress
+- Blocked
+- Under Review
+- Completed
+- Deferred
+- Cancelled
+
+---
+
+# Priority Levels
+
+Repository work SHALL use the following priorities:
+
+- Critical
+- High
+- Medium
+- Low
+
+Priority reflects implementation value, not implementation difficulty.
+
+---
+
+# Backlog Categories
+
+## Foundation
+
+Examples:
+
+- Repository configuration
+- Service interfaces
+- Common utilities
+- Configuration management
+
+---
+
+## Intake
+
+Examples:
+
+- Artifact intake service
+- Metadata generation
+- Artifact identification
+- Provenance creation
+
+---
+
+## Parsing
+
+Examples:
+
+- Markdown parser
+- PDF parser
+- Conversation parser
+- Code parser
+- Table parser
+
+---
+
+## Extraction
+
+Examples:
+
+- Asset extraction
+- Reference extraction
+- Timeline extraction
+- Metadata extraction
+
+---
+
+## Validation
+
+Examples:
+
+- Schema validation
+- Identifier validation
+- Relationship validation
+- Specification compliance
+
+---
+
+## Mapping
+
+Examples:
+
+- Duplicate detection
+- Artifact linking
+- Relationship discovery
+- Cross-reference generation
+
+---
+
+## Governance
+
+Examples:
+
+- Review workflow
+- Promotion workflow
+- Publication workflow
+- Approval recording
+
+---
+
+## Repository Services
+
+Examples:
+
+- Search
+- Index generation
+- Reporting
+- Analytics
+- Knowledge graph
+
+---
+
+## Automation
+
+Examples:
+
+- Scheduled ingestion
+- Automatic validation
+- Queue generation
+- Repository monitoring
+
+---
+
+# Standard Work Item
+
+Every backlog item SHALL include:
+
+- Work Item ID
+- Title
+- Description
+- Category
+- Priority
+- Status
+- Dependencies
+- Expected Outputs
+- Acceptance Criteria
+
+---
+
+# Acceptance Criteria
+
+A work item is complete only when:
+
+- Requirements are implemented.
+- Tests pass.
+- Documentation is updated.
+- Specifications remain satisfied.
+- No unresolved blockers remain.
+
+---
+
+# Dependency Management
+
+Dependencies SHALL be recorded explicitly.
+
+Circular dependencies SHOULD be avoided.
+
+Blocked work SHALL identify the blocking item.
+
+---
+
+# Progress Tracking
+
+Repository progress SHOULD be measured using:
+
+- Completed work items
+- Remaining work items
+- Phase completion
+- Specification coverage
+- Test coverage
+
+Implementation progress SHALL be evidence-based.
+
+---
+
+# Design Philosophy
+
+Specifications describe the destination.
+
+The backlog describes the journey.
+
+A disciplined backlog transforms architectural intent into working software.
+
+---
+
+End of Specification.
