+# Repository Maturity Model
+
+**Version:** 0.1  
+**Status:** Draft  
+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
+
+---
+
+# Purpose
+
+The Repository Maturity Model defines progressive levels of capability for the Repository Ingestion Framework.
+
+The model provides an objective means of assessing repository evolution over time.
+
+Maturity measures organizational capability rather than implementation size.
+
+---
+
+# Design Principles
+
+- Maturity is cumulative.
+- Higher levels build upon lower levels.
+- Assessment is evidence-based.
+- Capability is preferred over complexity.
+- Governance remains central at every level.
+
+---
+
+# Maturity Levels
+
+The Repository Ingestion Framework defines six maturity levels:
+
+- Level 0 — Ad Hoc
+- Level 1 — Managed
+- Level 2 — Structured
+- Level 3 — Governed
+- Level 4 — Integrated
+- Level 5 — Institutional
+
+---
+
+# Level 0 — Ad Hoc
+
+## Characteristics
+
+- Manual processes
+- No common schemas
+- Minimal documentation
+- Inconsistent organization
+- Limited repeatability
+
+## Typical State
+
+Artifacts exist but are not systematically managed.
+
+---
+
+# Level 1 — Managed
+
+## Characteristics
+
+- Repository structure established
+- Basic documentation
+- Artifact identification
+- Initial provenance
+- Manual governance
+
+## Success Indicators
+
+- Repository organization exists.
+- Artifact intake is consistent.
+
+---
+
+# Level 2 — Structured
+
+## Characteristics
+
+- Standard schemas
+- Service contracts
+- Structural parsing
+- Validation
+- Consistent metadata
+
+## Success Indicators
+
+- Repository objects follow common standards.
+- Parsing is deterministic.
+
+---
+
+# Level 3 — Governed
+
+## Characteristics
+
+- Review workflows
+- Promotion workflows
+- Publication workflows
+- Version governance
+- Architecture Decision Records
+
+## Success Indicators
+
+- Canonical knowledge is governed.
+- Repository history is preserved.
+
+---
+
+# Level 4 — Integrated
+
+## Characteristics
+
+- Cross-artifact relationships
+- Knowledge graph
+- Repository-wide search
+- Automated indexing
+- Automated validation
+
+## Success Indicators
+
+- Repository knowledge is interconnected.
+- Discovery is efficient.
+
+---
+
+# Level 5 — Institutional
+
+## Characteristics
+
+- Continuous ingestion
+- Repository analytics
+- Pattern discovery
+- Knowledge synthesis
+- Explainable recommendations
+
+## Success Indicators
+
+- Repository supports institutional reasoning.
+- Knowledge evolves through governance.
+- Provenance remains complete.
+
+---
+
+# Assessment Criteria
+
+Repository maturity SHOULD be evaluated using:
+
+- Specification coverage
+- Schema adoption
+- Test coverage
+- Validation success
+- Governance compliance
+- Automation level
+- Repository integrity
+- Documentation completeness
+
+Assessments SHOULD be repeatable.
+
+---
+
+# Advancement
+
+Progression between maturity levels SHOULD require objective evidence.
+
+Advancement SHALL NOT be based solely on implementation effort.
+
+Examples of acceptable evidence include:
+
+- Completed specifications
+- Passing validation reports
+- Test results
+- Governance records
+- Architecture Decision Records
+
+---
+
+# Continuous Improvement
+
+Maturity assessments SHOULD identify:
+
+- Strengths
+- Weaknesses
+- Risks
+- Improvement opportunities
+- Recommended priorities
+
+Assessment results SHOULD inform future implementation planning.
+
+---
+
+# Design Philosophy
+
+Repository maturity is measured by disciplined capability rather than accumulated features.
+
+The highest level of maturity is achieved when the repository consistently transforms evidence into governed institutional knowledge while preserving integrity, provenance, and explainability.
+
+---
+
+End of Specification.
\ No newline at end of file
