+# Repository Standards
+
+**Version:** 0.1  
+**Status:** Draft  
+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
+
+---
+
+# Purpose
+
+The Repository Standards define the common conventions that SHALL be followed throughout the Repository Ingestion Framework.
+
+These standards promote consistency across specifications, schemas, services, implementations, and documentation.
+
+Where a conflict exists, the Repository Constitution takes precedence.
+
+---
+
+# Design Principles
+
+- Consistency before convenience.
+- Explicitness before assumption.
+- Reproducibility before optimization.
+- Simplicity before complexity.
+- Standards before exceptions.
+
+---
+
+# Naming Standards
+
+Repository object names SHOULD be:
+
+- Descriptive
+- Stable
+- Singular where practical
+- Free of unnecessary abbreviations
+
+Examples:
+
+- Artifact
+- Asset
+- Provenance Record
+- Validation Report
+
+Names SHOULD communicate purpose rather than implementation.
+
+---
+
+# File Naming
+
+Repository files SHOULD use:
+
+- Pascal_Case
+- Underscores as separators
+- Meaningful names
+- Explicit version identifiers
+
+Example:
+
+Repository_Standards_v0.1.md
+
+---
+
+# Directory Organization
+
+Directories SHOULD group related functionality.
+
+Examples:
+
+- ingestion/
+- schemas/
+- services/
+- parsers/
+- governance/
+- validation/
+- documentation/
+
+Directory depth SHOULD remain reasonable.
+
+---
+
+# Versioning Standard
+
+Repository documents SHALL include:
+
+- Version
+- Status
+- Subsystem
+
+Version changes SHALL follow repository governance.
+
+---
+
+# Identifier Standards
+
+Identifiers SHALL be:
+
+- Globally unique where required.
+- Stable.
+- Machine-readable.
+- Human-referenceable.
+
+Identifiers SHALL NOT encode implementation details.
+
+---
+
+# Documentation Standard
+
+Every repository document SHOULD contain:
+
+- Title
+- Version
+- Status
+- Purpose
+- Body
+- Design Philosophy
+
+Normative documents SHOULD clearly distinguish requirements from guidance.
+
+---
+
+# Requirement Language
+
+The following terminology SHALL be interpreted consistently:
+
+- SHALL — mandatory requirement
+- SHALL NOT — prohibited behavior
+- SHOULD — recommended practice
+- SHOULD NOT — discouraged practice
+- MAY — optional capability
+
+Normative language SHALL be used intentionally.
+
+---
+
+# Formatting
+
+Repository documents SHOULD:
+
+- Use Markdown.
+- Use consistent heading levels.
+- Use readable spacing.
+- Prefer simple tables where appropriate.
+- Avoid unnecessary formatting complexity.
+
+Formatting SHOULD improve readability.
+
+---
+
+# Traceability
+
+Repository objects SHOULD reference related:
+
+- Specifications
+- Schemas
+- Service Contracts
+- ADRs
+- Repository Objects
+
+Cross-references improve institutional understanding.
+
+---
+
+# Error Reporting
+
+Errors SHOULD include:
+
+- Identifier
+- Description
+- Severity
+- Timestamp
+- Source Component
+
+Error records SHOULD support diagnosis without ambiguity.
+
+---
+
+# Logging Standards
+
+Logs SHOULD record:
+
+- Execution start
+- Execution end
+- Warnings
+- Errors
+- Processing duration
+
+Logs SHOULD avoid duplication of repository records.
+
+---
+
+# Testing Standards
+
+Repository implementations SHOULD maintain:
+
+- Repeatable tests
+- Independent tests
+- Version-controlled test artifacts
+- Automated execution where practical
+
+Testing standards SHALL align with the Testing Strategy Specification.
+
+---
+
+# Governance Standards
+
+Repository changes SHOULD:
+
+- Preserve provenance.
+- Preserve history.
+- Preserve compatibility where practical.
+- Undergo documented review.
+
+Governance applies equally to documentation and implementation.
+
+---
+
+# Design Philosophy
+
+Standards reduce unnecessary variation.
+
+Reduced variation improves maintainability.
+
+Maintainability enables institutional longevity.
+
+The Repository Standards provide the shared engineering discipline that allows independent components to function as a coherent whole.
+
+---
+
+End of Specification.
\ No newline at end of file
