+# Workflow Replay Specification
+
+**Version:** 0.1  
+**Status:** Draft  
+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
+
+---
+
+# Purpose
+
+The Workflow Replay Specification defines the canonical mechanisms for re-executing previously completed or partially completed workflows while preserving repository integrity, provenance, and reproducibility.
+
+Replay supports validation, testing, migration, auditing, debugging, and deterministic reconstruction of repository operations.
+
+---
+
+# Design Principles
+
+- Replay is deterministic.
+- Replay is explicitly initiated.
+- Replay preserves provenance.
+- Replay never alters historical records.
+- Replay is fully observable.
+
+---
+
+# Scope
+
+This specification applies to:
+
+- Workflow Orchestration
+- Workflow Execution
+- Event Processing
+- Validation
+- Repository Migration
+- Testing
+- Audit Operations
+
+---
+
+# Replay Definition
+
+A replay is the controlled re-execution of an existing workflow using previously recorded execution history, workflow definitions, and preserved execution context.
+
+Replay SHALL produce a new execution while maintaining a relationship to the original execution.
+
+---
+
+# Replay Objectives
+
+Replay SHALL enable:
+
+- Deterministic validation
+- Regression testing
+- Repository migration verification
+- Debugging
+- Audit verification
+- Historical reconstruction
+
+Replay SHALL NOT modify historical execution records.
+
+---
+
+# Replay Types
+
+Supported replay types include:
+
+| Replay Type | Description |
+|-------------|-------------|
+| Full Replay | Execute the complete workflow from the beginning |
+| Partial Replay | Execute a selected range of workflow steps |
+| Validation Replay | Execute for verification only |
+| Migration Replay | Validate repository migrations |
+| Diagnostic Replay | Investigate workflow behavior |
+| Simulation Replay | Execute without modifying repository state |
+
+Additional replay types MAY be defined through governance.
+
+---
+
+# Replay Prerequisites
+
+Replay SHALL require:
+
+- Approved Workflow Definition
+- Original Execution Record
+- Valid Workflow Context
+- Provenance Chain
+- Repository Compatibility
+
+Replay SHALL fail if prerequisites cannot be validated.
+
+---
+
+# Replay Inputs
+
+Replay SHALL identify:
+
+- Original Workflow ID
+- Original Execution ID
+- Replay Execution ID
+- Replay Type
+- Replay Initiator
+- Replay Reason
+- Replay Timestamp
+
+Replay inputs SHALL become part of execution history.
+
+---
+
+# Replay Context
+
+Replay SHALL reconstruct:
+
+- Workflow Context
+- Execution Parameters
+- Repository Context
+- Dependency State
+- Provenance References
+
+Replay SHALL preserve execution identity relationships.
+
+---
+
+# Replay Execution
+
+Replay SHALL:
+
+1. Validate replay eligibility.
+2. Restore execution context.
+3. Create a new execution.
+4. Execute approved workflow steps.
+5. Compare outputs where applicable.
+6. Record replay results.
+
+Replay SHALL generate standard workflow events.
+
+---
+
+# Historical Preservation
+
+Replay SHALL NOT:
+
+- Replace historical executions.
+- Modify historical checkpoints.
+- Alter historical provenance.
+- Delete original events.
+
+Replay creates additional institutional history rather than replacing existing history.
+
+---
+
+# Output Comparison
+
+Replay MAY compare:
+
+- Generated artifacts
+- Validation results
+- Execution duration
+- Event sequences
+- Produced metadata
+- Repository state
+
+Comparison results SHALL be recorded.
+
+---
+
+# Replay Relationships
+
+Every replay SHALL maintain references to:
+
+- Original Workflow
+- Original Execution
+- Original Checkpoints
+- Original Provenance Chain
+
+Relationship records SHALL remain immutable.
+
+---
+
+# Simulation Mode
+
+Simulation Replay SHALL:
+
+- Execute workflow logic.
+- Generate execution reports.
+- Publish simulation events.
+- Avoid persistent repository modification.
+
+Simulation SHALL clearly identify all generated artifacts as non-production.
+
+---
+
+# Replay Events
+
+Replay SHALL publish events including:
+
+- Replay Requested
+- Replay Started
+- Replay Context Restored
+- Replay Step Completed
+- Replay Validation Completed
+- Replay Finished
+- Replay Failed
+
+Replay events SHALL conform to the Event Model Specification.
+
+---
+
+# Validation
+
+Replay SHALL validate:
+
+- Workflow compatibility
+- Schema compatibility
+- Repository consistency
+- Provenance continuity
+- Input completeness
+- Dependency availability
+
+Validation failures SHALL terminate replay.
+
+---
+
+# Monitoring
+
+Replay services SHOULD expose:
+
+- Replay count
+- Replay duration
+- Replay success rate
+- Validation failures
+- Simulation executions
+- Output comparison results
+
+Operational metrics SHALL support repository quality assurance.
+
+---
+
+# Repository Integration
+
+Workflow Replay integrates with:
+
+- Workflow Execution
+- Workflow Recovery
+- Workflow Checkpointing
+- Workflow Context
+- Event Processing
+- Provenance
+- Validation
+- Repository Indexes
+- Observability
+
+Replay SHALL preserve repository integrity and institutional history.
+
+---
+
+# Design Philosophy
+
+Replay is not repetition.
+
+It is the disciplined reconstruction of prior execution under controlled conditions to verify that institutional knowledge remains reproducible across time, implementations, and repository evolution.
+
+By treating replay as a governed capability rather than an ad hoc operation, the Repository Ingestion Framework ensures that every workflow can be independently verified without compromising the authenticity of historical records.
+
+---
+
+End of Specification.
