+# Workflow Service Configuration Specification
+
+**Version:** 0.1  
+**Status:** Draft  
+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
+
+---
+
+# Purpose
+
+The Workflow Service Configuration Specification defines the canonical model for configuring workflow services throughout their lifecycle.
+
+Configuration provides the controlled mechanism by which service behavior is adapted to different repository environments without altering service implementation.
+
+---
+
+# Design Principles
+
+- Configuration is declarative.
+- Configuration is versioned.
+- Configuration is externally managed.
+- Configuration is independently validated.
+- Configuration preserves provenance.
+- Configuration changes are auditable.
+
+---
+
+# Scope
+
+This specification applies to:
+
+- Workflow Services
+- Parser Services
+- Validation Services
+- Mapping Services
+- Repository Services
+- Event Services
+- Governance Services
+- Utility Services
+- Administrative Services
+
+---
+
+# Configuration Definition
+
+Service Configuration is a governed collection of parameters that influences service behavior while preserving the service's contractual responsibilities.
+
+Configuration SHALL modify operational behavior without changing service identity or capabilities.
+
+---
+
+# Configuration Objectives
+
+Configuration SHALL provide:
+
+- Environment-specific behavior
+- Operational flexibility
+- Controlled customization
+- Deployment consistency
+- Governance support
+- Operational reproducibility
+
+---
+
+# Canonical Configuration Schema
+
+Every configuration SHALL contain:
+
+| Field | Required | Description |
+|--------|----------|-------------|
+| Configuration ID | Yes | Unique configuration identifier |
+| Configuration Name | Yes | Human-readable name |
+| Configuration Version | Yes | Semantic version |
+| Service ID | Yes | Associated service |
+| Configuration Scope | Yes | Applicable execution scope |
+| Parameters | Yes | Defined configuration values |
+| Validation Rules | Yes | Parameter validation requirements |
+| Effective Date | Yes | Activation timestamp |
+| Status | Yes | Lifecycle state |
+| Provenance ID | Yes | Provenance reference |
+| Metadata | No | Supplemental configuration information |
+
+---
+
+# Configuration Identifier
+
+Configuration identifiers SHALL:
+
+- Be globally unique.
+- Never be reused.
+- Remain immutable.
+- Support repository indexing.
+
+Example:
+
+CFG-SVC-000247
+
+---
+
+# Configuration Scope
+
+Configuration MAY apply to:
+
+- Repository
+- Environment
+- Workflow
+- Service
+- Capability
+- Execution
+- Administrative Operations
+
+Scope SHALL be explicitly declared.
+
+---
+
+# Configuration Parameters
+
+Parameters MAY include:
+
+- Timeouts
+- Retry limits
+- Resource thresholds
+- Logging levels
+- Feature flags
+- Queue limits
+- Cache policies
+- Validation strictness
+- Publication behavior
+
+Parameters SHALL possess explicit types and validation rules.
+
+---
+
+# Parameter Types
+
+Supported parameter types include:
+
+- String
+- Integer
+- Decimal
+- Boolean
+- Enumeration
+- Duration
+- Collection
+- Structured Object
+
+Parameter definitions SHALL remain schema compliant.
+
+---
+
+# Default Values
+
+Parameters MAY define default values.
+
+Defaults SHALL:
+
+- Be explicitly documented.
+- Be deterministic.
+- Be applied consistently.
+- Remain version controlled.
+
+Implicit defaults SHALL NOT be permitted.
+
+---
+
+# Configuration Lifecycle
+
+Configurations SHALL progress through:
+
+Draft
+
+↓
+
+Review
+
+↓
+
+Approved
+
+↓
+
+Active
+
+↓
+
+Superseded
+
+↓
+
+Archived
+
+Lifecycle transitions SHALL follow repository governance.
+
+---
+
+# Configuration Activation
+
+Activation SHALL require:
+
+- Successful validation
+- Governance approval
+- Compatibility verification
+- Provenance assignment
+- Audit recording
+
+Activation SHALL NOT interrupt executing workflows unless explicitly authorized.
+
+---
+
+# Version Compatibility
+
+Configurations SHALL declare compatibility with:
+
+- Service versions
+- Workflow versions
+- Contract versions
+- Schema versions
+- Repository versions
+
+Incompatible configurations SHALL NOT become Active.
+
+---
+
+# Configuration Validation
+
+Configuration validation SHALL verify:
+
+- Schema compliance
+- Parameter types
+- Required parameters
+- Value constraints
+- Compatibility requirements
+- Governance approval
+
+Invalid configurations SHALL be rejected.
+
+---
+
+# Configuration Changes
+
+Configuration modifications SHALL:
+
+- Create a new configuration version.
+- Preserve historical configurations.
+- Record change justification.
+- Preserve provenance.
+- Generate audit events.
+
+Historical configurations SHALL remain immutable.
+
+---
+
+# Configuration Rollback
+
+Rollback SHALL restore a previously approved configuration version.
+
+Rollback SHALL:
+
+- Preserve execution history.
+- Record rollback events.
+- Validate compatibility.
+- Maintain provenance continuity.
+
+Rollback SHALL NOT modify historical configuration records.
+
+---
+
+# Configuration Events
+
+Configuration operations SHALL publish events including:
+
+- Configuration Created
+- Configuration Validated
+- Configuration Approved
+- Configuration Activated
+- Configuration Updated
+- Configuration Rolled Back
+- Configuration Archived
+
+Events SHALL conform to the Event Model Specification.
+
+---
+
+# Audit Requirements
+
+Configuration management SHALL record:
+
+- Configuration ID
+- Service ID
+- Version
+- Parameter changes
+- Validation results
+- Activation history
+- Responsible actor
+- Provenance references
+
+Audit records SHALL remain immutable.
+
+---
+
+# Monitoring
+
+Configuration services SHOULD expose:
+
+- Active configurations
+- Pending approvals
+- Validation failures
+- Configuration changes
+- Rollback frequency
+- Configuration compatibility issues
+
+Monitoring SHALL support operational governance.
+
+---
+
+# Security
+
+Configuration services SHALL:
+
+- Enforce authorization.
+- Protect sensitive parameters.
+- Validate configuration integrity.
+- Record administrative access.
+- Preserve confidentiality where required.
+
+Sensitive configuration values SHALL be handled according to repository security policies.
+
+---
+
+# Repository Integration
+
+Workflow Service Configuration integrates with:
+
+- Workflow Service Registry
+- Workflow Service Lifecycle
+- Workflow Service Contracts
+- Workflow Service Dependencies
+- Workflow Orchestration
+- Configuration Management
+- Repository Indexes
+- Event Processing
+- Observability
+
+Configuration management SHALL preserve repository integrity and institutional accountability.
+
+---
+
+# Design Philosophy
+
+Service behavior should evolve through governed configuration rather than uncontrolled implementation changes.
+
+By treating configuration as a first-class, versioned repository asset, the Repository Ingestion Framework enables reproducible deployments, controlled operational flexibility, and long-term maintainability while preserving determinism, provenance, and institutional trust.
+
+---
+
+End of Specification.
