+# Workflow Service Dependency Specification
+
+**Version:** 0.1  
+**Status:** Draft  
+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
+
+---
+
+# Purpose
+
+The Workflow Service Dependency Specification defines the canonical model for declaring, validating, governing, and monitoring dependencies between services participating in the Repository Ingestion Framework.
+
+Dependency management ensures that services interact predictably while maintaining deterministic execution, architectural integrity, and institutional governance.
+
+---
+
+# Design Principles
+
+- Dependencies are explicit.
+- Dependencies are version-aware.
+- Dependencies are deterministic.
+- Dependencies are independently validatable.
+- Dependencies preserve provenance.
+- Circular dependencies are prohibited.
+
+---
+
+# Scope
+
+This specification applies to:
+
+- Workflow Services
+- Parser Services
+- Validation Services
+- Repository Services
+- Event Services
+- Governance Services
+- Utility Services
+- Administrative Services
+
+---
+
+# Dependency Definition
+
+A dependency is a formal relationship in which one service requires the capability, contract, resource, or availability of another service to perform its declared responsibilities.
+
+Dependencies SHALL be declared explicitly.
+
+---
+
+# Dependency Objectives
+
+Dependency management SHALL provide:
+
+- Architectural transparency
+- Execution planning
+- Compatibility verification
+- Failure isolation
+- Recovery planning
+- Governance support
+
+---
+
+# Canonical Dependency Schema
+
+Every dependency declaration SHALL contain:
+
+| Field | Required | Description |
+|--------|----------|-------------|
+| Dependency ID | Yes | Unique dependency identifier |
+| Consumer Service | Yes | Service requiring the dependency |
+| Provider Service | Yes | Service providing the dependency |
+| Dependency Type | Yes | Dependency classification |
+| Required Capability | Yes | Required capability identifier |
+| Required Contract | Yes | Required service contract |
+| Minimum Version | No | Minimum compatible version |
+| Maximum Version | No | Maximum supported version |
+| Optional | Yes | Indicates whether dependency is optional |
+| Validation Rules | Yes | Dependency validation criteria |
+| Provenance ID | Yes | Provenance reference |
+| Metadata | No | Supplemental dependency information |
+
+---
+
+# Dependency Identifier
+
+Dependency identifiers SHALL:
+
+- Be globally unique.
+- Remain immutable.
+- Never be reused.
+- Support repository indexing.
+
+Example:
+
+DEP-SVC-001245
+
+---
+
+# Dependency Categories
+
+Supported dependency types include:
+
+- Service Dependency
+- Contract Dependency
+- Capability Dependency
+- Event Dependency
+- Repository Dependency
+- Schema Dependency
+- Configuration Dependency
+- External System Dependency
+
+Additional dependency categories MAY be established through governance.
+
+---
+
+# Mandatory Dependencies
+
+Mandatory dependencies SHALL:
+
+- Be satisfied before execution.
+- Pass compatibility validation.
+- Support required contracts.
+- Preserve workflow determinism.
+
+Failure of a mandatory dependency SHALL prevent workflow execution.
+
+---
+
+# Optional Dependencies
+
+Optional dependencies MAY enhance functionality without preventing execution.
+
+Optional dependencies SHALL:
+
+- Be explicitly identified.
+- Have defined fallback behavior.
+- Never introduce inconsistent execution.
+
+---
+
+# Dependency Resolution
+
+Dependency resolution SHALL proceed in the following order:
+
+1. Validate dependency declaration.
+2. Locate provider service.
+3. Verify provider lifecycle state.
+4. Verify capability compatibility.
+5. Verify contract compatibility.
+6. Verify version compatibility.
+7. Record dependency resolution.
+
+Resolution SHALL be deterministic.
+
+---
+
+# Dependency Graph
+
+All dependencies SHALL form a directed dependency graph.
+
+The dependency graph SHALL:
+
+- Be acyclic.
+- Be version-aware.
+- Support visualization.
+- Support impact analysis.
+- Support execution planning.
+
+Cycles SHALL be rejected during validation.
+
+---
+
+# Dependency Compatibility
+
+Compatibility SHALL verify:
+
+- Service lifecycle state
+- Service contract version
+- Capability version
+- Workflow compatibility
+- Schema compatibility
+- Repository compatibility
+
+Compatibility SHALL be evaluated before execution.
+
+---
+
+# Dependency Health
+
+Dependency health SHOULD be classified as:
+
+- Healthy
+- Degraded
+- Unavailable
+- Deprecated
+- Unsupported
+
+Health classifications SHALL support operational decision-making.
+
+---
+
+# Dependency Changes
+
+Changes to dependencies SHALL require:
+
+- Compatibility assessment
+- Impact analysis
+- Governance review
+- Validation
+- Audit recording
+
+Dependency changes SHALL NOT invalidate historical executions.
+
+---
+
+# Failure Handling
+
+Dependency failures SHALL:
+
+- Record diagnostics.
+- Publish dependency events.
+- Preserve execution history.
+- Support workflow recovery where applicable.
+
+Dependency failures SHALL NOT corrupt repository state.
+
+---
+
+# Monitoring
+
+Dependency management SHOULD expose:
+
+- Total dependencies
+- Active dependencies
+- Optional dependencies
+- Failed dependency resolutions
+- Compatibility violations
+- Dependency health
+- Circular dependency violations
+
+Monitoring SHALL support operational governance.
+
+---
+
+# Audit Requirements
+
+Every dependency operation SHALL record:
+
+- Dependency ID
+- Consumer service
+- Provider service
+- Validation result
+- Compatibility assessment
+- Timestamp
+- Responsible actor
+- Provenance reference
+
+Audit records SHALL remain immutable.
+
+---
+
+# Validation
+
+Dependency validation SHALL verify:
+
+- Identifier uniqueness
+- Provider existence
+- Consumer existence
+- Capability compatibility
+- Contract compatibility
+- Version compatibility
+- Lifecycle compatibility
+- Graph acyclicity
+
+Invalid dependencies SHALL NOT become Active.
+
+---
+
+# Repository Integration
+
+Workflow Service Dependency Management integrates with:
+
+- Workflow Service Registry
+- Workflow Service Discovery
+- Workflow Service Contracts
+- Workflow Service Capabilities
+- Workflow Service Lifecycle
+- Workflow Orchestration
+- Event Processing
+- Repository Indexes
+- Observability
+
+Dependency management SHALL preserve repository integrity and institutional accountability.
+
+---
+
+# Design Philosophy
+
+Services should cooperate through well-defined relationships rather than implicit assumptions.
+
+By modeling dependencies as governed, versioned, and auditable repository assets, the Repository Ingestion Framework enables reliable orchestration, controlled evolution, and resilient execution while preventing architectural drift and hidden coupling.
+
+---
+
+End of Specification.
