+# Workflow Service Load Balancing Specification
+
+**Version:** 0.1  
+**Status:** Draft  
+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
+
+---
+
+# Purpose
+
+The Workflow Service Load Balancing Specification defines the canonical architecture for distributing workflow execution requests across multiple service instances while preserving deterministic execution, service contracts, repository integrity, and operational governance.
+
+Load balancing provides efficient utilization of service capacity, improves availability, and supports scalable execution without affecting functional behavior.
+
+---
+
+# Design Principles
+
+- Load distribution is deterministic.
+- Load balancing is policy-driven.
+- Service contracts remain unchanged.
+- Execution affinity is preserved where required.
+- Load balancing decisions are observable.
+- Load distribution is fully auditable.
+
+---
+
+# Scope
+
+This specification applies to:
+
+- Workflow Services
+- Parser Services
+- Validation Services
+- Mapping Services
+- Repository Services
+- Event Services
+- Governance Services
+- Utility Services
+- Administrative Services
+
+---
+
+# Load Balancing Definition
+
+Load Balancing is the governed distribution of execution requests across multiple compatible service instances according to approved routing policies.
+
+Load balancing SHALL optimize resource utilization while preserving workflow correctness and repository consistency.
+
+---
+
+# Load Balancing Objectives
+
+Load balancing SHALL provide:
+
+- Even workload distribution
+- High service availability
+- Reduced execution latency
+- Resource optimization
+- Fault isolation
+- Operational resilience
+
+---
+
+# Canonical Load Balancing Schema
+
+Every load balancing policy SHALL contain:
+
+| Field | Required | Description |
+|--------|----------|-------------|
+| Policy ID | Yes | Unique load balancing policy identifier |
+| Service ID | Yes | Associated service |
+| Policy Version | Yes | Semantic version |
+| Routing Strategy | Yes | Distribution algorithm |
+| Instance Selection Rules | Yes | Selection criteria |
+| Health Requirements | Yes | Instance health criteria |
+| Affinity Policy | Yes | Session/workflow affinity rules |
+| Failover Policy | Yes | Alternate routing behavior |
+| Validation Rules | Yes | Policy validation requirements |
+| Provenance ID | Yes | Provenance reference |
+| Metadata | No | Supplemental policy information |
+
+---
+
+# Routing Strategies
+
+Supported routing strategies include:
+
+- Round Robin
+- Weighted Round Robin
+- Least Connections
+- Least Load
+- Resource-Aware
+- Workflow Affinity
+- Capability-Based
+- Priority-Based
+- Randomized (Governed)
+
+Additional strategies MAY be introduced through governance.
+
+---
+
+# Instance Selection
+
+Instance selection SHALL evaluate:
+
+- Health status
+- Lifecycle state
+- Capability compatibility
+- Contract compatibility
+- Resource availability
+- Current workload
+
+Only Healthy and Active service instances SHALL receive production workload.
+
+---
+
+# Workflow Affinity
+
+Workflow affinity SHALL ensure that related execution requests are consistently routed according to defined policies.
+
+Affinity MAY be based upon:
+
+- Workflow ID
+- Execution ID
+- Correlation ID
+- Repository Context
+- Tenant Identifier
+- Administrative Scope
+
+Affinity rules SHALL be deterministic.
+
+---
+
+# Health-Aware Routing
+
+Load balancing SHALL consider health information during routing.
+
+Instances classified as:
+
+- Healthy SHALL receive workload.
+- Degraded MAY receive workload according to policy.
+- Recovering SHALL be excluded unless explicitly permitted.
+- Unhealthy SHALL NOT receive workload.
+- Offline SHALL NOT receive workload.
+
+Health evaluation SHALL integrate with the Workflow Service Health Specification.
+
+---
+
+# Capacity Awareness
+
+Routing decisions SHOULD consider:
+
+- CPU utilization
+- Memory utilization
+- Queue depth
+- Active executions
+- Response latency
+- Historical throughput
+
+Capacity metrics SHALL be obtained from governed monitoring systems.
+
+---
+
+# Failover
+
+Failover SHALL occur when:
+
+- A service instance becomes unavailable.
+- Health validation fails.
+- Timeout thresholds are exceeded.
+- Administrative maintenance begins.
+- Dependency failures prevent execution.
+
+Failover SHALL preserve execution integrity whenever possible.
+
+---
+
+# Retry Routing
+
+When retries are required, routing policies SHALL define whether execution SHALL:
+
+- Return to the original instance
+- Select a new compatible instance
+- Escalate to recovery
+- Await dependency restoration
+
+Retry routing SHALL preserve workflow determinism.
+
+---
+
+# Routing Constraints
+
+Load balancing SHALL respect:
+
+- Service contracts
+- Capability declarations
+- Version compatibility
+- Dependency requirements
+- Repository boundaries
+- Security policies
+
+Constraint violations SHALL prevent routing.
+
+---
+
+# Load Distribution Metrics
+
+Load balancing SHOULD monitor:
+
+- Instance utilization
+- Queue length
+- Execution latency
+- Routing success rate
+- Failed routing attempts
+- Failover frequency
+- Capacity utilization
+- Affinity consistency
+
+Metrics SHALL support operational governance.
+
+---
+
+# Validation
+
+Load balancing SHALL validate:
+
+- Service registration
+- Instance health
+- Policy compliance
+- Routing eligibility
+- Version compatibility
+- Repository connectivity
+
+Invalid routing decisions SHALL be rejected.
+
+---
+
+# Load Balancing Events
+
+Load balancing SHALL publish events including:
+
+- Routing Requested
+- Instance Selected
+- Routing Completed
+- Routing Failed
+- Failover Initiated
+- Failover Completed
+- Policy Updated
+- Affinity Established
+
+Events SHALL conform to the Event Model Specification.
+
+---
+
+# Audit Requirements
+
+Load balancing operations SHALL record:
+
+- Policy ID
+- Service ID
+- Selected instance
+- Routing strategy
+- Health assessment
+- Routing timestamp
+- Responsible component
+- Provenance reference
+
+Audit records SHALL remain immutable.
+
+---
+
+# Security
+
+Load balancing SHALL:
+
+- Enforce authorized routing policies.
+- Prevent unauthorized instance selection.
+- Validate service identity.
+- Preserve execution confidentiality.
+- Record privileged administrative changes.
+
+Security validation SHALL occur before workload distribution.
+
+---
+
+# Monitoring
+
+Operational monitoring SHOULD expose:
+
+- Active service instances
+- Routing frequency
+- Distribution balance
+- Failover events
+- Health-based exclusions
+- Affinity compliance
+- Routing latency
+- Policy violations
+
+Monitoring SHALL support operational visibility and capacity planning.
+
+---
+
+# Repository Integration
+
+Workflow Service Load Balancing integrates with:
+
+- Workflow Service Registry
+- Workflow Service Discovery
+- Workflow Service Health
+- Workflow Service Scaling
+- Workflow Service Resilience
+- Workflow Service Dependencies
+- Workflow Orchestration
+- Workflow Execution
+- Observability
+- Event Processing
+- Repository Indexes
+
+Load balancing SHALL preserve repository integrity, deterministic execution, and institutional accountability.
+
+---
+
+# Design Philosophy
+
+Load balancing is an architectural coordination capability rather than a networking implementation detail.
+
+By governing routing decisions through explicit policies, validated health information, and auditable execution records, the Repository Ingestion Framework ensures that workload distribution remains predictable, reproducible, scalable, and consistent with the framework's principles of determinism, provenance, and long-term operational governance.
+
+---
+
+End of Specification.
