+# Workflow Service Routing Specification
+
+**Version:** 0.1  
+**Status:** Draft  
+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
+
+---
+
+# Purpose
+
+The Workflow Service Routing Specification defines the canonical architecture for routing workflow requests, messages, events, and execution context between services participating in the Repository Ingestion Framework.
+
+Routing determines how work is directed through the service ecosystem while preserving deterministic execution, contractual integrity, repository consistency, and institutional governance.
+
+---
+
+# Design Principles
+
+- Routing is deterministic.
+- Routing is policy-driven.
+- Routing is contract-aware.
+- Routing decisions are observable.
+- Routing preserves provenance.
+- Routing is independently auditable.
+
+---
+
+# Scope
+
+This specification applies to:
+
+- Workflow Services
+- Parser Services
+- Validation Services
+- Mapping Services
+- Repository Services
+- Publication Services
+- Event Services
+- Governance Services
+- Utility Services
+- Administrative Services
+
+---
+
+# Routing Definition
+
+Workflow Service Routing is the governed process of selecting the appropriate destination service or execution path for requests, messages, events, and workflow activities based upon approved routing policies.
+
+Routing SHALL remain independent of transport technologies and implementation details.
+
+---
+
+# Routing Objectives
+
+Routing SHALL provide:
+
+- Deterministic destination selection
+- Capability-aware routing
+- Version-aware routing
+- Policy enforcement
+- Failure-aware routing
+- Operational transparency
+
+---
+
+# Canonical Routing Schema
+
+Every routing policy SHALL contain:
+
+| Field | Required | Description |
+|--------|----------|-------------|
+| Routing Policy ID | Yes | Unique routing policy identifier |
+| Routing Name | Yes | Human-readable policy name |
+| Policy Version | Yes | Semantic version |
+| Routing Scope | Yes | Applicable routing domain |
+| Source Definition | Yes | Originating service or workflow |
+| Destination Criteria | Yes | Destination selection rules |
+| Routing Strategy | Yes | Routing methodology |
+| Fallback Policy | Yes | Alternate routing behavior |
+| Validation Rules | Yes | Routing validation requirements |
+| Provenance ID | Yes | Provenance reference |
+| Metadata | No | Supplemental routing information |
+
+---
+
+# Routing Scope
+
+Routing MAY apply to:
+
+- Workflow Execution
+- Service Invocation
+- Event Distribution
+- Message Delivery
+- Administrative Operations
+- Repository Operations
+- Recovery Operations
+
+Routing scope SHALL be explicitly declared.
+
+---
+
+# Routing Strategies
+
+Supported routing strategies include:
+
+- Static Routing
+- Capability-Based Routing
+- Policy-Based Routing
+- Priority Routing
+- Dependency-Aware Routing
+- Context-Aware Routing
+- Health-Aware Routing
+- Hybrid Routing
+
+Additional routing strategies MAY be established through governance.
+
+---
+
+# Destination Selection
+
+Destination selection SHALL evaluate:
+
+- Service identity
+- Capability declaration
+- Contract compatibility
+- Lifecycle state
+- Health status
+- Version compatibility
+- Dependency availability
+
+Only eligible services SHALL receive routed work.
+
+---
+
+# Capability-Based Routing
+
+Capability-based routing SHALL direct work toward services that declare compatible capabilities.
+
+Capability evaluation SHALL verify:
+
+- Capability identifier
+- Capability version
+- Capability lifecycle
+- Capability compatibility
+
+Routing SHALL fail if no compatible capability exists.
+
+---
+
+# Context-Aware Routing
+
+Routing MAY consider execution context including:
+
+- Workflow ID
+- Execution ID
+- Correlation ID
+- Repository Context
+- Security Context
+- Tenant Context
+- Administrative Context
+
+Context SHALL remain consistent throughout execution.
+
+---
+
+# Policy Evaluation
+
+Routing policies SHALL evaluate:
+
+- Governance policies
+- Scheduling constraints
+- Security requirements
+- Repository rules
+- Resource availability
+- Operational priorities
+
+Policy evaluation SHALL complete before destination selection.
+
+---
+
+# Fallback Routing
+
+Fallback routing SHALL define behavior when the preferred destination cannot accept work.
+
+Fallback MAY include:
+
+- Alternate compatible service
+- Deferred execution
+- Queue placement
+- Recovery workflow
+- Administrative escalation
+
+Fallback SHALL preserve workflow correctness.
+
+---
+
+# Routing Constraints
+
+Routing SHALL enforce:
+
+- Service contract compliance
+- Capability compatibility
+- Dependency validation
+- Repository boundaries
+- Security policies
+- Lifecycle compatibility
+
+Constraint violations SHALL prevent routing.
+
+---
+
+# Dynamic Routing
+
+Dynamic routing MAY adapt to operational conditions while remaining deterministic.
+
+Dynamic routing MAY consider:
+
+- Service health
+- Current workload
+- Dependency availability
+- Resource utilization
+- Maintenance status
+
+Routing decisions SHALL remain reproducible from recorded operational evidence.
+
+---
+
+# Validation
+
+Routing validation SHALL verify:
+
+- Policy integrity
+- Destination eligibility
+- Contract compatibility
+- Capability compatibility
+- Repository connectivity
+- Security authorization
+
+Invalid routing decisions SHALL be rejected.
+
+---
+
+# Routing Events
+
+Routing SHALL publish events including:
+
+- Routing Requested
+- Routing Evaluated
+- Destination Selected
+- Routing Completed
+- Routing Failed
+- Fallback Activated
+- Routing Policy Updated
+- Routing Policy Archived
+
+Events SHALL conform to the Event Model Specification.
+
+---
+
+# Audit Requirements
+
+Routing activities SHALL record:
+
+- Routing Policy ID
+- Source
+- Destination
+- Routing strategy
+- Policy evaluation
+- Validation results
+- Timestamp
+- Provenance reference
+
+Audit records SHALL remain immutable.
+
+---
+
+# Monitoring
+
+Routing services SHOULD expose:
+
+- Routing requests
+- Successful routing decisions
+- Failed routing attempts
+- Fallback activations
+- Destination utilization
+- Policy violations
+- Routing latency
+- Routing throughput
+
+Monitoring SHALL support operational governance and optimization.
+
+---
+
+# Security
+
+Routing SHALL:
+
+- Authenticate requesting services.
+- Authorize routing operations.
+- Validate destination eligibility.
+- Protect execution context.
+- Record privileged routing actions.
+
+Unauthorized routing SHALL be prohibited.
+
+---
+
+# Repository Integration
+
+Workflow Service Routing integrates with:
+
+- Workflow Service Registry
+- Workflow Service Discovery
+- Workflow Service Communication
+- Workflow Service Messaging
+- Workflow Service Coordination
+- Workflow Service Dependencies
+- Workflow Service Health
+- Workflow Execution
+- Workflow Orchestration
+- Event Processing
+- Observability
+- Repository Indexes
+
+Routing SHALL preserve repository integrity, deterministic execution, and institutional accountability.
+
+---
+
+# Design Philosophy
+
+Routing is the decision-making layer that transforms architectural intent into operational execution.
+
+By treating routing policies as governed, versioned, and auditable repository assets, the Repository Ingestion Framework ensures that every execution path is deterministic, explainable, reproducible, and aligned with service capabilities, governance policies, and long-term architectural evolution.
+
+---
+
+End of Specification.
