+# Workflow Service Scaling Specification
+
+**Version:** 0.1  
+**Status:** Draft  
+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
+
+---
+
+# Purpose
+
+The Workflow Service Scaling Specification defines the canonical architecture for scaling workflow services to meet changing operational demands while preserving deterministic execution, service contracts, repository integrity, and governance.
+
+Scaling enables the Repository Ingestion Framework to support increasing workloads, concurrent executions, and repository growth without compromising reliability or auditability.
+
+---
+
+# Design Principles
+
+- Scaling is policy-driven.
+- Scaling is deterministic.
+- Scaling preserves service identity.
+- Scaling is observable.
+- Scaling decisions are auditable.
+- Scaling shall not alter functional behavior.
+
+---
+
+# Scope
+
+This specification applies to:
+
+- Workflow Services
+- Parser Services
+- Validation Services
+- Mapping Services
+- Repository Services
+- Event Services
+- Governance Services
+- Utility Services
+- Administrative Services
+
+---
+
+# Scaling Definition
+
+Service Scaling is the governed adjustment of service execution capacity to accommodate changing workload demands while maintaining declared service capabilities and contractual behavior.
+
+Scaling SHALL preserve operational consistency across all service instances.
+
+---
+
+# Scaling Objectives
+
+Service scaling SHALL provide:
+
+- Increased execution capacity
+- Predictable performance
+- Resource efficiency
+- Operational elasticity
+- Controlled concurrency
+- Repository stability
+
+---
+
+# Canonical Scaling Schema
+
+Every scaling policy SHALL contain:
+
+| Field | Required | Description |
+|--------|----------|-------------|
+| Scaling Policy ID | Yes | Unique scaling policy identifier |
+| Service ID | Yes | Associated service |
+| Policy Version | Yes | Semantic version |
+| Scaling Strategy | Yes | Scaling methodology |
+| Minimum Capacity | Yes | Minimum service instances |
+| Maximum Capacity | Yes | Maximum service instances |
+| Scaling Triggers | Yes | Trigger conditions |
+| Cooldown Policy | Yes | Stabilization intervals |
+| Resource Limits | Yes | Resource boundaries |
+| Provenance ID | Yes | Provenance reference |
+| Metadata | No | Supplemental policy information |
+
+---
+
+# Scaling Strategies
+
+Supported scaling strategies include:
+
+- Horizontal Scaling
+- Vertical Scaling
+- Scheduled Scaling
+- Demand-Based Scaling
+- Predictive Scaling
+- Manual Scaling
+
+Scaling strategies SHALL be explicitly declared.
+
+---
+
+# Horizontal Scaling
+
+Horizontal scaling increases or decreases the number of service instances.
+
+Horizontal scaling SHALL:
+
+- Preserve stateless execution where possible.
+- Maintain contract compatibility.
+- Preserve execution determinism.
+- Support load distribution.
+
+---
+
+# Vertical Scaling
+
+Vertical scaling adjusts the resources assigned to an existing service instance.
+
+Vertical scaling MAY adjust:
+
+- CPU allocation
+- Memory allocation
+- Storage allocation
+- Network bandwidth
+
+Vertical scaling SHALL preserve service identity.
+
+---
+
+# Scheduled Scaling
+
+Scheduled scaling SHALL activate according to predefined operational schedules.
+
+Schedules MAY account for:
+
+- Business hours
+- Batch processing windows
+- Repository maintenance
+- Expected ingestion peaks
+
+Schedules SHALL be version controlled.
+
+---
+
+# Demand-Based Scaling
+
+Demand-based scaling SHALL respond to measurable operational indicators.
+
+Indicators MAY include:
+
+- Queue depth
+- Request rate
+- Active workflows
+- Resource utilization
+- Response latency
+- Processing backlog
+
+Scaling decisions SHALL follow configured thresholds.
+
+---
+
+# Predictive Scaling
+
+Predictive scaling MAY utilize historical operational data to anticipate workload changes.
+
+Predictive policies SHALL:
+
+- Remain explainable.
+- Be independently configurable.
+- Preserve deterministic activation criteria.
+- Record prediction rationale.
+
+---
+
+# Scaling Triggers
+
+Scaling MAY be triggered by:
+
+- CPU utilization
+- Memory utilization
+- Queue depth
+- Concurrent workflows
+- Response time
+- Event backlog
+- Repository throughput
+- Administrative request
+
+Every trigger SHALL define measurable thresholds.
+
+---
+
+# Capacity Limits
+
+Every scaling policy SHALL define:
+
+- Minimum capacity
+- Maximum capacity
+- Reserved capacity
+- Emergency capacity
+
+Capacity limits SHALL prevent uncontrolled resource consumption.
+
+---
+
+# Cooldown Policy
+
+Cooldown periods SHALL prevent excessive scaling activity.
+
+Cooldown policies SHALL specify:
+
+- Scale-out stabilization interval
+- Scale-in stabilization interval
+- Minimum observation period
+- Trigger reevaluation interval
+
+Cooldown behavior SHALL be deterministic.
+
+---
+
+# Load Distribution
+
+When multiple service instances exist, workload distribution SHALL:
+
+- Be deterministic.
+- Preserve execution context.
+- Maintain workflow affinity where required.
+- Avoid duplicate processing.
+
+Load balancing mechanisms SHALL remain implementation-independent.
+
+---
+
+# State Management
+
+Scaling SHALL preserve:
+
+- Workflow state
+- Execution context
+- Correlation identifiers
+- Checkpoints
+- Provenance
+- Audit records
+
+State SHALL remain consistent regardless of scaling activity.
+
+---
+
+# Dependency Considerations
+
+Scaling SHALL evaluate:
+
+- Dependency capacity
+- Dependency compatibility
+- Service discovery updates
+- Resource availability
+- Repository connectivity
+
+Scaling SHALL NOT overwhelm dependent services.
+
+---
+
+# Validation
+
+Scaling operations SHALL validate:
+
+- Service health
+- Resource availability
+- Policy compliance
+- Dependency readiness
+- Repository connectivity
+- Contract compatibility
+
+Validation SHALL precede scaling actions.
+
+---
+
+# Scaling Events
+
+Scaling operations SHALL publish events including:
+
+- Scaling Requested
+- Scaling Approved
+- Scale-Out Started
+- Scale-Out Completed
+- Scale-In Started
+- Scale-In Completed
+- Scaling Failed
+- Scaling Policy Updated
+
+Events SHALL conform to the Event Model Specification.
+
+---
+
+# Audit Requirements
+
+Scaling activities SHALL record:
+
+- Service ID
+- Scaling policy
+- Trigger condition
+- Capacity before scaling
+- Capacity after scaling
+- Timestamp
+- Responsible component
+- Provenance reference
+
+Audit records SHALL remain immutable.
+
+---
+
+# Monitoring
+
+Scaling services SHOULD expose:
+
+- Current capacity
+- Scaling frequency
+- Resource utilization
+- Queue depth
+- Active workflows
+- Scaling failures
+- Cooldown status
+- Capacity trends
+
+Monitoring SHALL support operational governance and capacity planning.
+
+---
+
+# Security
+
+Scaling operations SHALL:
+
+- Require authorized administrative control.
+- Validate scaling policies.
+- Protect against resource exhaustion attacks.
+- Preserve configuration integrity.
+- Record privileged operations.
+
+Unauthorized scaling SHALL be prohibited.
+
+---
+
+# Repository Integration
+
+Workflow Service Scaling integrates with:
+
+- Workflow Service Health
+- Workflow Service Resilience
+- Workflow Service Configuration
+- Workflow Service Discovery
+- Workflow Service Registry
+- Workflow Orchestration
+- Workflow Execution
+- Observability
+- Event Processing
+- Repository Indexes
+
+Scaling management SHALL preserve repository integrity and institutional accountability.
+
+---
+
+# Design Philosophy
+
+Scalability is an architectural capability that enables sustainable repository growth without altering service behavior.
+
+By treating scaling policies as governed repository assets, the Repository Ingestion Framework ensures that operational elasticity remains predictable, observable, reproducible, and fully auditable while preserving deterministic workflow execution and long-term institutional stability.
+
+---
+
+End of Specification.
