+# Workflow Service Scheduling Specification
+
+**Version:** 0.1  
+**Status:** Draft  
+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
+
+---
+
+# Purpose
+
+The Workflow Service Scheduling Specification defines the canonical architecture for scheduling workflow service execution within the Repository Ingestion Framework.
+
+Scheduling governs when services execute, under what conditions execution begins, how execution windows are managed, and how scheduling policies preserve deterministic repository operations.
+
+---
+
+# Design Principles
+
+- Scheduling is declarative.
+- Scheduling is deterministic.
+- Scheduling policies are versioned.
+- Scheduling decisions are observable.
+- Scheduling preserves provenance.
+- Scheduling is independently auditable.
+
+---
+
+# Scope
+
+This specification applies to:
+
+- Workflow Services
+- Parser Services
+- Validation Services
+- Mapping Services
+- Repository Services
+- Publication Services
+- Governance Services
+- Utility Services
+- Administrative Services
+
+---
+
+# Scheduling Definition
+
+Service Scheduling is the governed process of determining when eligible workflow services are permitted to execute according to approved scheduling policies.
+
+Scheduling SHALL coordinate execution timing without modifying service behavior.
+
+---
+
+# Scheduling Objectives
+
+Scheduling SHALL provide:
+
+- Predictable execution timing
+- Controlled resource utilization
+- Execution prioritization
+- Operational consistency
+- Maintenance coordination
+- Governance compliance
+
+---
+
+# Canonical Scheduling Schema
+
+Every scheduling policy SHALL contain:
+
+| Field | Required | Description |
+|--------|----------|-------------|
+| Schedule ID | Yes | Unique scheduling policy identifier |
+| Service ID | Yes | Associated service |
+| Schedule Version | Yes | Semantic version |
+| Schedule Type | Yes | Scheduling methodology |
+| Trigger Definition | Yes | Execution trigger |
+| Execution Window | Yes | Permitted execution period |
+| Priority | Yes | Execution priority |
+| Concurrency Policy | Yes | Concurrent execution rules |
+| Exception Policy | Yes | Exceptional scheduling behavior |
+| Provenance ID | Yes | Provenance reference |
+| Metadata | No | Supplemental scheduling information |
+
+---
+
+# Schedule Types
+
+Supported scheduling types include:
+
+- Immediate
+- On Demand
+- Fixed Interval
+- Calendar-Based
+- Event-Driven
+- Dependency-Driven
+- Queue-Driven
+- Administrative
+- Hybrid
+
+Additional scheduling types MAY be introduced through governance.
+
+---
+
+# Trigger Definition
+
+Scheduling triggers MAY include:
+
+- Time-based events
+- Repository events
+- Workflow completion
+- Dependency availability
+- Queue thresholds
+- Administrative requests
+- External notifications
+- Manual approval
+
+Triggers SHALL be explicitly defined.
+
+---
+
+# Execution Windows
+
+Execution windows SHALL specify:
+
+- Start time
+- End time
+- Time zone
+- Allowed execution periods
+- Maintenance exclusions
+- Blackout periods
+
+Execution outside approved windows SHALL be prohibited unless governance explicitly authorizes an exception.
+
+---
+
+# Priority Levels
+
+Scheduling priorities SHALL include:
+
+- Critical
+- High
+- Normal
+- Low
+- Background
+
+Priority SHALL influence execution ordering but SHALL NOT bypass governance requirements.
+
+---
+
+# Concurrency Policy
+
+Scheduling SHALL define whether services MAY execute:
+
+- Exclusively
+- Concurrently
+- Sequentially
+- Parallel with limitations
+- Dependency-controlled
+
+Concurrency SHALL preserve repository consistency.
+
+---
+
+# Queue Management
+
+Scheduling SHOULD support execution queues.
+
+Queue policies MAY define:
+
+- Queue capacity
+- Ordering strategy
+- Priority handling
+- Expiration rules
+- Retry behavior
+
+Queue processing SHALL remain deterministic.
+
+---
+
+# Dependency Scheduling
+
+Services dependent upon other services SHALL execute only after:
+
+- Dependency completion
+- Dependency validation
+- Dependency health verification
+- Dependency compatibility verification
+
+Dependency scheduling SHALL integrate with the Workflow Service Dependency Specification.
+
+---
+
+# Maintenance Windows
+
+Scheduling SHALL recognize maintenance periods.
+
+Maintenance windows MAY:
+
+- Suspend execution
+- Delay execution
+- Allow administrative workflows
+- Prevent new workflow initiation
+
+Maintenance schedules SHALL be governed through Configuration Management.
+
+---
+
+# Exception Policy
+
+Scheduling policies SHALL define behavior for:
+
+- Missed schedules
+- Delayed execution
+- Resource shortages
+- Dependency failures
+- Administrative overrides
+
+Exception handling SHALL preserve provenance and auditability.
+
+---
+
+# Execution Ordering
+
+Where multiple scheduled executions compete for resources, ordering SHALL consider:
+
+- Priority
+- Scheduled execution time
+- Dependency completion
+- Resource availability
+- Governance constraints
+
+Ordering SHALL remain deterministic.
+
+---
+
+# Schedule Validation
+
+Scheduling validation SHALL verify:
+
+- Policy integrity
+- Trigger validity
+- Execution window correctness
+- Dependency readiness
+- Resource availability
+- Repository connectivity
+
+Invalid schedules SHALL NOT be activated.
+
+---
+
+# Schedule Changes
+
+Schedule modifications SHALL:
+
+- Produce a new schedule version.
+- Preserve historical schedules.
+- Record governance approval.
+- Preserve provenance.
+- Generate audit events.
+
+Historical schedules SHALL remain immutable.
+
+---
+
+# Scheduling Events
+
+Scheduling SHALL publish events including:
+
+- Schedule Created
+- Schedule Updated
+- Schedule Activated
+- Execution Scheduled
+- Execution Deferred
+- Execution Started
+- Execution Completed
+- Schedule Suspended
+- Schedule Archived
+
+Events SHALL conform to the Event Model Specification.
+
+---
+
+# Audit Requirements
+
+Scheduling operations SHALL record:
+
+- Schedule ID
+- Service ID
+- Trigger
+- Execution time
+- Priority
+- Validation outcome
+- Responsible component
+- Provenance reference
+
+Audit records SHALL remain immutable.
+
+---
+
+# Monitoring
+
+Scheduling services SHOULD expose:
+
+- Scheduled executions
+- Missed schedules
+- Deferred executions
+- Queue utilization
+- Execution latency
+- Priority distribution
+- Scheduling conflicts
+- Policy violations
+
+Monitoring SHALL support operational governance and planning.
+
+---
+
+# Security
+
+Scheduling SHALL:
+
+- Enforce authorized schedule modification.
+- Validate administrative requests.
+- Prevent unauthorized execution.
+- Protect scheduling policies.
+- Record privileged scheduling actions.
+
+Scheduling SHALL comply with repository security policies.
+
+---
+
+# Repository Integration
+
+Workflow Service Scheduling integrates with:
+
+- Workflow Orchestration
+- Workflow Execution
+- Workflow Service Registry
+- Workflow Service Discovery
+- Workflow Service Dependencies
+- Workflow Service Configuration
+- Workflow Service Health
+- Workflow Service Scaling
+- Event Processing
+- Observability
+- Repository Indexes
+
+Scheduling SHALL preserve repository integrity, execution determinism, and institutional accountability.
+
+---
+
+# Design Philosophy
+
+Scheduling transforms workflow execution from an ad hoc operational activity into a governed architectural capability.
+
+By representing schedules as versioned, validated, and auditable repository assets, the Repository Ingestion Framework ensures that execution timing remains predictable, reproducible, and fully aligned with governance, operational readiness, and long-term institutional management.
+
+---
+
+End of Specification.
