+# Workflow Versioning Specification
+
+**Version:** 0.1  
+**Status:** Draft  
+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
+
+---
+
+# Purpose
+
+The Workflow Versioning Specification defines the canonical approach for identifying, managing, evolving, and governing workflow definitions throughout their lifecycle.
+
+Versioning enables workflows to evolve without compromising reproducibility, provenance, or historical execution records.
+
+---
+
+# Design Principles
+
+- Versions are immutable.
+- Version identifiers are deterministic.
+- Historical versions remain executable where supported.
+- Version evolution is governed.
+- Version relationships are explicit.
+
+---
+
+# Scope
+
+This specification applies to:
+
+- Workflow Definitions
+- Workflow Schemas
+- Workflow Execution
+- Workflow Replay
+- Workflow Recovery
+- Workflow Governance
+- Repository Publication
+
+---
+
+# Version Definition
+
+A workflow version is an immutable representation of an approved workflow definition.
+
+Each published version SHALL uniquely identify:
+
+- Workflow structure
+- Step definitions
+- Dependencies
+- Inputs
+- Outputs
+- Execution behavior
+
+---
+
+# Version Identifier
+
+Every workflow SHALL possess:
+
+- Workflow ID
+- Workflow Version
+
+Version identifiers SHALL uniquely identify a specific workflow definition.
+
+Example:
+
+Workflow ID:
+
+WF-INGEST-001
+
+Workflow Version:
+
+1.0.0
+
+---
+
+# Version Format
+
+Workflow versions SHALL follow Semantic Versioning.
+
+Format:
+
+MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH
+
+Example:
+
+1.0.0
+
+1.2.3
+
+2.0.0
+
+---
+
+# Major Version
+
+A Major version SHALL increment when changes introduce incompatibility.
+
+Examples include:
+
+- Workflow restructuring
+- Breaking input changes
+- Breaking output changes
+- State model changes
+- Execution behavior changes
+
+Major version changes MAY require migration.
+
+---
+
+# Minor Version
+
+A Minor version SHALL increment when functionality is added without breaking compatibility.
+
+Examples include:
+
+- Additional optional steps
+- Additional metadata
+- New optional outputs
+- Enhanced validation
+
+Minor versions SHOULD remain backward compatible.
+
+---
+
+# Patch Version
+
+A Patch version SHALL increment when corrections are made without changing workflow behavior.
+
+Examples include:
+
+- Documentation corrections
+- Metadata improvements
+- Clarifications
+- Non-functional refinements
+
+Patch releases SHALL preserve execution compatibility.
+
+---
+
+# Version Lifecycle
+
+Workflow versions SHALL progress through:
+
+Draft
+
+↓
+
+Review
+
+↓
+
+Approved
+
+↓
+
+Active
+
+↓
+
+Deprecated
+
+↓
+
+Archived
+
+Lifecycle transitions SHALL follow repository governance policies.
+
+---
+
+# Active Versions
+
+Multiple workflow versions MAY remain Active simultaneously.
+
+When multiple versions are active:
+
+- Each SHALL remain independently executable.
+- Each SHALL possess separate documentation.
+- Each SHALL maintain independent provenance.
+
+---
+
+# Version Compatibility
+
+Compatibility SHALL be classified as:
+
+| Compatibility | Description |
+|---------------|-------------|
+| Fully Compatible | Existing executions continue unchanged |
+| Backward Compatible | Older executions remain supported |
+| Forward Compatible | Newer definitions tolerate older inputs |
+| Incompatible | Migration required |
+
+Compatibility SHALL be explicitly documented.
+
+---
+
+# Version Selection
+
+Workflow execution SHALL determine the workflow version using:
+
+1. Explicit version request
+2. Repository policy
+3. Default active version
+
+Version selection SHALL be deterministic.
+
+---
+
+# Historical Executions
+
+Historical workflow executions SHALL remain permanently associated with the workflow version under which they were executed.
+
+Historical executions SHALL NOT automatically migrate.
+
+---
+
+# Version Migration
+
+Migration between workflow versions SHALL:
+
+- Preserve provenance.
+- Preserve execution history.
+- Preserve workflow identity.
+- Record migration events.
+
+Migration SHALL NOT overwrite previous workflow definitions.
+
+---
+
+# Deprecation
+
+Deprecated workflow versions:
+
+- SHALL remain identifiable.
+- SHALL remain historically searchable.
+- MAY restrict new executions.
+- SHALL preserve execution history.
+
+Deprecation SHALL NOT delete workflow definitions.
+
+---
+
+# Archival
+
+Archived workflow versions SHALL:
+
+- Remain immutable.
+- Preserve provenance.
+- Support replay where permitted.
+- Support auditing.
+
+Archived versions SHALL NOT evolve.
+
+---
+
+# Validation
+
+Workflow version validation SHALL verify:
+
+- Semantic version format
+- Identifier uniqueness
+- Compatibility classification
+- Schema validity
+- Dependency consistency
+- Governance approval
+
+Invalid versions SHALL NOT become Active.
+
+---
+
+# Monitoring
+
+Workflow version management SHOULD expose:
+
+- Active versions
+- Deprecated versions
+- Archived versions
+- Version adoption
+- Migration activity
+- Compatibility issues
+
+Version metrics SHALL support governance decisions.
+
+---
+
+# Repository Integration
+
+Workflow Versioning integrates with:
+
+- Workflow Definition Schema
+- Workflow Execution
+- Workflow Replay
+- Workflow Recovery
+- Workflow Governance
+- Schema Governance
+- Repository Indexes
+- Lifecycle Management
+
+Version management SHALL preserve repository integrity.
+
+---
+
+# Design Philosophy
+
+A workflow definition represents institutional knowledge at a specific point in time.
+
+Versioning allows that knowledge to evolve deliberately while ensuring that every historical execution remains reproducible, explainable, and permanently attributable to the exact definition under which it was performed.
+
+By separating workflow identity from workflow version, the Repository Ingestion Framework enables continuous improvement without sacrificing historical fidelity.
+
+---
+
+End of Specification.
