-+# Architecture Decision Record (ADR) Specification
-+
-+**Version:** 0.1  
-+**Status:** Draft  
-+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Purpose
-+
-+The Architecture Decision Record (ADR) Specification defines the standard for documenting significant architectural decisions within the Repository Ingestion Framework.
-+
-+Architectural decisions become part of the institutional memory of the repository.
-+
-+Every significant decision SHALL be documented.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Principles
-+
-+- Decisions are explicit.
-+- Rationale is preserved.
-+- Alternatives are recorded.
-+- Decisions remain traceable.
-+- History is immutable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Scope
-+
-+Architecture Decision Records SHALL document decisions affecting:
-+
-+- Repository architecture
-+- Data models
-+- Service boundaries
-+- Schemas
-+- Processing pipelines
-+- Governance processes
-+- Infrastructure
-+- External dependencies
-+
-+Minor implementation details SHOULD NOT require ADRs.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# ADR Lifecycle
-+
-+Each ADR SHALL exist in one of the following states:
-+
-+- Proposed
-+- Under Review
-+- Accepted
-+- Superseded
-+- Deprecated
-+- Rejected
-+
-+Lifecycle changes SHALL be recorded.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Standard ADR Structure
-+
-+Every ADR SHALL include:
-+
-+- ADR Identifier
-+- Title
-+- Status
-+- Date
-+- Authors
-+- Related Specifications
-+- Related ADRs
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Context
-+
-+The Context section SHALL describe:
-+
-+- Existing situation
-+- Problem statement
-+- Constraints
-+- Assumptions
-+
-+The context SHALL explain why a decision is required.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Decision
-+
-+The Decision section SHALL clearly state:
-+
-+- What was decided.
-+- Scope of the decision.
-+- Intended outcome.
-+
-+The decision SHALL be stated unambiguously.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Rationale
-+
-+The Rationale section SHALL explain:
-+
-+- Why the decision was selected.
-+- Expected benefits.
-+- Trade-offs.
-+- Risks considered.
-+
-+Rationale SHALL outlive implementation details.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Alternatives Considered
-+
-+Every ADR SHOULD record reasonable alternatives.
-+
-+Each alternative SHOULD include:
-+
-+- Description
-+- Advantages
-+- Disadvantages
-+- Reason for rejection
-+
-+Rejected alternatives remain valuable historical evidence.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Consequences
-+
-+The Consequences section SHALL identify:
-+
-+- Expected impacts
-+- Required implementation changes
-+- Future considerations
-+- Compatibility implications
-+
-+Consequences SHOULD include both positive and negative outcomes.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Dependencies
-+
-+Each ADR SHOULD reference:
-+
-+- Related specifications
-+- Related schemas
-+- Related services
-+- Related ADRs
-+
-+Dependencies improve repository traceability.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Supersession
-+
-+When an ADR is replaced:
-+
-+- The previous ADR SHALL remain accessible.
-+- The replacement ADR SHALL reference its predecessor.
-+- Historical context SHALL be preserved.
-+
-+Architectural history SHALL NOT be rewritten.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Repository Integration
-+
-+Architecture Decision Records SHOULD be indexed by:
-+
-+- ADR Identifier
-+- Status
-+- Topic
-+- Date
-+- Related Components
-+
-+Repository search SHOULD support ADR discovery.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Review
-+
-+Accepted ADRs SHOULD undergo periodic review to determine whether:
-+
-+- The decision remains appropriate.
-+- New constraints have emerged.
-+- Supersession is warranted.
-+
-+Review SHALL preserve historical records.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Philosophy
-+
-+Architecture is not merely the collection of current decisions.
-+
-+It is the recorded history of why those decisions were made.
-+
-+Architecture Decision Records transform individual reasoning into institutional knowledge.
-+
-+---
-+
-+End of Specification.
-\ No newline at end of file
