-+# Developer Guide
-+
-+**Version:** 0.1  
-+**Status:** Draft  
-+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Purpose
-+
-+The Developer Guide provides implementation guidance for engineers building and maintaining the Repository Ingestion Framework.
-+
-+Unlike specifications, this guide is informative rather than normative.
-+
-+Specifications remain the authoritative source for repository behavior.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Intended Audience
-+
-+This document is intended for:
-+
-+- Repository developers
-+- Parser developers
-+- Service developers
-+- Repository maintainers
-+- Future contributors
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Development Principles
-+
-+Repository development SHOULD prioritize:
-+
-+- Simplicity
-+- Readability
-+- Determinism
-+- Testability
-+- Maintainability
-+
-+Complexity SHOULD only be introduced when justified by measurable benefit.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Repository Philosophy
-+
-+The ingestion framework exists to transform evidence into governed institutional knowledge.
-+
-+Every implementation decision SHOULD preserve:
-+
-+- Provenance
-+- Reproducibility
-+- Repository integrity
-+- Deterministic behavior
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Recommended Development Order
-+
-+Implementation SHOULD proceed in the following order:
-+
-+1. Service contracts
-+2. Data schemas
-+3. Core models
-+4. Artifact intake
-+5. Structural parsers
-+6. Asset extraction
-+7. Validation
-+8. Repository mapping
-+9. Governance workflows
-+10. Repository services
-+
-+Each completed component SHOULD remain operational as new components are introduced.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Coding Guidelines
-+
-+Repository code SHOULD:
-+
-+- Have a single responsibility.
-+- Use descriptive names.
-+- Avoid hidden side effects.
-+- Favor composition over inheritance.
-+- Minimize shared mutable state.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Error Handling
-+
-+Errors SHOULD:
-+
-+- Be explicit.
-+- Preserve repository state.
-+- Preserve evidence.
-+- Record diagnostic information.
-+- Support recovery.
-+
-+Errors SHALL NOT silently discard repository data.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Logging
-+
-+Repository logging SHOULD record:
-+
-+- Service execution
-+- Validation failures
-+- Lifecycle transitions
-+- Repository events
-+- Unexpected conditions
-+
-+Logs SHOULD aid debugging without becoming repository artifacts.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Testing Strategy
-+
-+Every component SHOULD include:
-+
-+- Unit tests
-+- Integration tests
-+- Regression tests
-+
-+Where practical, tests SHOULD be deterministic and repeatable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Documentation
-+
-+Every implementation SHOULD include:
-+
-+- Purpose
-+- Inputs
-+- Outputs
-+- Dependencies
-+- Known limitations
-+
-+Public interfaces SHOULD be documented before implementation is considered complete.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Version Compatibility
-+
-+Implementations SHOULD declare:
-+
-+- Supported schema versions
-+- Supported specification versions
-+- Compatibility notes
-+
-+Breaking changes SHOULD be documented before release.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Repository Integrity
-+
-+Developers SHALL avoid:
-+
-+- Direct modification of canonical artifacts
-+- Destructive updates
-+- Loss of provenance
-+- Hidden transformations
-+
-+Repository history is considered institutional memory.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Contributing
-+
-+Before introducing new functionality, contributors SHOULD determine whether:
-+
-+- An existing specification already governs the behavior.
-+- A new specification is required.
-+- Existing schemas require revision.
-+- Additional tests are necessary.
-+
-+Implementation SHOULD follow governance, not replace it.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Philosophy
-+
-+Specifications establish the constitution.
-+
-+Implementations realize the constitution.
-+
-+Developers are stewards of repository integrity.
-+
-+---
-+
-+End of Specification.
-\ No newline at end of file
