-+# Event Schema Specification
-+
-+**Version:** 0.1
-+**Status:** Draft
-+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Purpose
-+
-+The Event Schema Specification defines the canonical data structure for repository events.
-+
-+The Event Model defines how events behave.
-+
-+The Event Catalog defines what events exist.
-+
-+This specification defines how events are represented.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Principles
-+
-+- Every event has a single schema.
-+- Schemas are versioned.
-+- Schemas are deterministic.
-+- Schemas preserve provenance.
-+- Schemas support validation.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Canonical Event Schema
-+
-+Every event SHALL contain the following fields.
-+
-+| Field | Required | Description |
-+|--------|----------|-------------|
-+| Event ID | Yes | Globally unique event identifier |
-+| Event Type | Yes | Event name defined by the Event Catalog |
-+| Event Category | Yes | Logical event grouping |
-+| Event Version | Yes | Schema version |
-+| Timestamp | Yes | Time event occurred |
-+| Source Component | Yes | Service producing the event |
-+| Subject ID | Yes | Identifier of affected repository object |
-+| Correlation ID | No | Workflow identifier |
-+| Parent Event ID | No | Parent event |
-+| Provenance ID | Yes | Provenance record |
-+| Lifecycle State | No | Current lifecycle state |
-+| Severity | No | Informational, Warning, Error, Critical |
-+| Payload | No | Event-specific data |
-+| Diagnostics | No | Processing diagnostics |
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Event Identifier
-+
-+Every Event ID SHALL:
-+
-+- Be globally unique.
-+- Never be reused.
-+- Remain immutable.
-+- Be machine-readable.
-+
-+Example:
-+
-+EVT-2026-000001
-+
-+Identifier format MAY evolve under governance.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Timestamp
-+
-+Timestamps SHALL:
-+
-+- Use UTC.
-+- Follow ISO-8601.
-+- Record creation time.
-+
-+Example:
-+
-+2026-07-28T18:43:15Z
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Event Type
-+
-+Event Type SHALL match a definition contained within the Event Catalog Specification.
-+
-+Example values:
-+
-+- Artifact Imported
-+- Parsing Completed
-+- Validation Failed
-+- Publication Completed
-+
-+Unknown event types SHALL fail validation.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Event Category
-+
-+Allowed categories include:
-+
-+- Intake
-+- Parser
-+- Asset
-+- Reference
-+- Validation
-+- Mapping
-+- Queue
-+- Lifecycle
-+- Governance
-+- Publication
-+- System
-+
-+Future categories SHALL be governed.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Source Component
-+
-+The producing component SHALL identify itself.
-+
-+Examples:
-+
-+- Artifact Intake Service
-+- Markdown Parser
-+- Validation Engine
-+- Repository Mapper
-+- Publication Service
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Subject Identifier
-+
-+Subject ID references the repository object affected by the event.
-+
-+Examples:
-+
-+- Artifact ID
-+- Asset ID
-+- Queue Candidate ID
-+- Relationship ID
-+
-+Exactly one primary subject SHALL be identified.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Correlation Identifier
-+
-+Correlation IDs group related events into a single workflow.
-+
-+Example workflow:
-+
-+Artifact Imported
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Parsing Completed
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Assets Extracted
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Validation Passed
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Publication Completed
-+
-+Each event shares the same Correlation ID.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Provenance
-+
-+Every event SHALL reference a Provenance ID.
-+
-+Events SHALL become part of the provenance chain.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Severity
-+
-+Permitted severity values:
-+
-+- Informational
-+- Warning
-+- Error
-+- Critical
-+
-+Severity describes operational significance.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Payload
-+
-+Payload MAY contain event-specific information.
-+
-+Payload SHALL:
-+
-+- Conform to schema.
-+- Avoid duplicate metadata.
-+- Preserve determinism.
-+
-+Payload SHALL NOT replace repository artifacts.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Diagnostics
-+
-+Diagnostics MAY include:
-+
-+- Warning messages
-+- Error codes
-+- Execution duration
-+- Validation details
-+
-+Diagnostics support operational analysis.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Validation Rules
-+
-+Every event SHALL validate:
-+
-+- Required fields present.
-+- Event Type exists.
-+- Category valid.
-+- Timestamp valid.
-+- Version supported.
-+- Identifiers correctly formatted.
-+- Provenance reference exists.
-+
-+Invalid events SHALL be rejected.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Versioning
-+
-+Schema revisions SHALL:
-+
-+- Preserve backward compatibility where practical.
-+- Record change history.
-+- Increment schema version.
-+- Document migration guidance.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Repository Integration
-+
-+Events SHALL integrate with:
-+
-+- Event Catalog
-+- Event Model
-+- Provenance
-+- Lifecycle
-+- Validation
-+- Observability
-+- Repository Indexes
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Philosophy
-+
-+Events are the repository's operational memory.
-+
-+The Event Schema provides the structure that allows every operation to be recorded consistently, validated objectively, and understood long after the implementation that generated it has evolved.
-+
-+---
-+
-+End of Specification.# Event Schema Specification
