-+# Workflow API Specification
-+
-+**Version:** 0.1  
-+**Status:** Draft  
-+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Purpose
-+
-+The Workflow API Specification defines the canonical interfaces for interacting with workflow services within the Repository Ingestion Framework.
-+
-+The API provides a stable contract between clients, services, orchestration engines, automation systems, and administrative tools.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Principles
-+
-+- APIs are resource-oriented.
-+- APIs are versioned.
-+- APIs are deterministic.
-+- APIs preserve provenance.
-+- APIs are implementation-independent.
-+- APIs are backward compatible whenever practical.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Scope
-+
-+This specification applies to:
-+
-+- Workflow Definition Services
-+- Workflow Execution Services
-+- Workflow Management Services
-+- Workflow Monitoring Services
-+- Workflow Governance Services
-+- Administrative Services
-+
-+---
-+
-+# API Architecture
-+
-+Workflow APIs SHALL expose capabilities through well-defined service endpoints.
-+
-+API implementations SHOULD remain stateless.
-+
-+Workflow state SHALL be maintained by repository services rather than client sessions.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# API Versioning
-+
-+Every API SHALL expose:
-+
-+- API Version
-+- Supported Workflow Versions
-+- Supported Schema Versions
-+
-+API versioning SHALL follow Semantic Versioning.
-+
-+Example:
-+
-+v1.0.0
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Resource Model
-+
-+Primary workflow resources include:
-+
-+- Workflows
-+- Executions
-+- Checkpoints
-+- Replays
-+- Recoveries
-+- Audit Records
-+- Metrics
-+- Reports
-+
-+Additional resources MAY be introduced through governance.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Standard Operations
-+
-+Workflow resources SHOULD support the following operations where applicable:
-+
-+| Operation | Description |
-+|-----------|-------------|
-+| Create | Create a resource |
-+| Retrieve | Read a resource |
-+| Update | Modify mutable resource attributes |
-+| Delete | Administrative removal where permitted |
-+| List | Enumerate resources |
-+| Search | Locate matching resources |
-+| Validate | Validate a resource |
-+| Execute | Start workflow execution |
-+| Replay | Replay a workflow |
-+| Recover | Recover a workflow |
-+| Cancel | Cancel execution |
-+| Archive | Archive eligible resources |
-+
-+Deletion SHALL follow repository governance policies.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Resource Identifiers
-+
-+Every resource SHALL possess a globally unique identifier.
-+
-+Examples include:
-+
-+- Workflow ID
-+- Execution ID
-+- Checkpoint ID
-+- Audit ID
-+- Report ID
-+
-+Identifiers SHALL remain immutable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Request Requirements
-+
-+Every request SHOULD include:
-+
-+- API Version
-+- Correlation ID
-+- Request Timestamp
-+- Authentication Context
-+- Requested Resource
-+
-+Optional request metadata MAY include execution parameters.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Response Requirements
-+
-+Every response SHOULD include:
-+
-+- Response Status
-+- Correlation ID
-+- Timestamp
-+- Requested Resource
-+- Schema Version
-+- Diagnostic Information (if applicable)
-+
-+Responses SHALL be deterministic.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Error Handling
-+
-+Errors SHALL return structured information including:
-+
-+- Error Code
-+- Error Message
-+- Error Category
-+- Correlation ID
-+- Diagnostic Metadata
-+- Recovery Guidance (where appropriate)
-+
-+Errors SHALL NOT expose sensitive implementation details.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Workflow Execution API
-+
-+Execution services SHOULD support:
-+
-+- Execute Workflow
-+- Query Execution Status
-+- Suspend Execution
-+- Resume Execution
-+- Cancel Execution
-+- Retrieve Execution History
-+
-+Execution operations SHALL preserve provenance.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Workflow Definition API
-+
-+Definition services SHOULD support:
-+
-+- Register Workflow
-+- Retrieve Workflow
-+- Validate Workflow
-+- Publish Workflow
-+- Deprecate Workflow
-+- Archive Workflow
-+
-+Definition changes SHALL follow governance approval.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Workflow Recovery API
-+
-+Recovery services SHOULD support:
-+
-+- Request Recovery
-+- Validate Recovery Eligibility
-+- Resume Workflow
-+- Query Recovery Status
-+- Retrieve Recovery History
-+
-+Recovery SHALL preserve execution continuity.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Workflow Replay API
-+
-+Replay services SHOULD support:
-+
-+- Initiate Replay
-+- Query Replay Status
-+- Compare Replay Results
-+- Retrieve Replay History
-+
-+Replay SHALL create a new execution record.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Monitoring API
-+
-+Monitoring services SHOULD expose:
-+
-+- Active Workflows
-+- Queue Depth
-+- Execution Metrics
-+- Service Health
-+- Recovery Statistics
-+- Repository Health
-+
-+Monitoring SHALL support operational dashboards.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Security
-+
-+Workflow APIs SHALL support:
-+
-+- Authentication
-+- Authorization
-+- Audit Logging
-+- Request Validation
-+- Rate Limiting
-+- Secure Communication
-+
-+Security SHALL comply with repository security policies.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Validation
-+
-+API requests SHALL validate:
-+
-+- Schema compliance
-+- Resource identifiers
-+- Authentication context
-+- Authorization rules
-+- Request integrity
-+- Repository consistency
-+
-+Invalid requests SHALL be rejected.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Observability
-+
-+API interactions SHALL generate:
-+
-+- Request events
-+- Response events
-+- Error events
-+- Performance metrics
-+- Audit records
-+
-+Observability SHALL support operational analysis.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Repository Integration
-+
-+Workflow APIs integrate with:
-+
-+- Workflow Execution
-+- Workflow Governance
-+- Workflow Versioning
-+- Workflow Audit
-+- Workflow Metrics
-+- Workflow Reporting
-+- Event Processing
-+- Repository Indexes
-+- Observability
-+
-+API operations SHALL preserve repository integrity and institutional accountability.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Philosophy
-+
-+The Workflow API is the canonical interface between repository capabilities and their consumers.
-+
-+By exposing stable, governed, and versioned contracts, the Repository Ingestion Framework enables independent implementation, interoperability, automation, and long-term evolution while preserving determinism, provenance, and institutional trust.
-+
-+---
-+
-+End of Specification.
-\ No newline at end of file
