-+# Workflow Checkpointing Specification
-+
-+**Version:** 0.1  
-+**Status:** Draft  
-+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Purpose
-+
-+The Workflow Checkpointing Specification defines the canonical mechanism for preserving workflow execution progress to support deterministic recovery, auditing, and long-running workflow execution.
-+
-+Checkpointing ensures that completed work does not require unnecessary repetition following interruption or failure.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Principles
-+
-+- Checkpoints are deterministic.
-+- Checkpoints are immutable.
-+- Checkpoints preserve provenance.
-+- Checkpoints support recovery.
-+- Checkpoints are independently auditable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Scope
-+
-+This specification applies to:
-+
-+- Workflow Orchestration
-+- Workflow Execution
-+- Recovery Services
-+- Long-running Workflows
-+- Repository Operations
-+- Automation Services
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Checkpoint Definition
-+
-+A checkpoint is an immutable record describing a workflow's execution state at a specific point in time.
-+
-+A checkpoint SHALL enable execution to resume from a known valid state.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Canonical Checkpoint Schema
-+
-+Every checkpoint SHALL contain the following fields.
-+
-+| Field | Required | Description |
-+|--------|----------|-------------|
-+| Checkpoint ID | Yes | Unique checkpoint identifier |
-+| Workflow ID | Yes | Associated workflow |
-+| Execution ID | Yes | Associated execution |
-+| Correlation ID | Yes | Shared execution correlation |
-+| Workflow State | Yes | State at checkpoint creation |
-+| Completed Step | Yes | Most recently completed step |
-+| Timestamp | Yes | Checkpoint creation time |
-+| Provenance ID | Yes | Provenance reference |
-+| Repository Context | Yes | Repository execution context |
-+| Validation Status | Yes | Checkpoint validation outcome |
-+| Metadata | No | Supplemental execution information |
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Checkpoint Identifier
-+
-+Checkpoint IDs SHALL:
-+
-+- Be globally unique.
-+- Never be reused.
-+- Remain immutable.
-+- Support repository indexing.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Checkpoint Creation
-+
-+Workflow engines SHOULD create checkpoints:
-+
-+- Before workflow execution
-+- After successful step completion
-+- Before external dependencies
-+- Before human review
-+- Before publication
-+- Prior to suspension
-+- Prior to cancellation
-+- Immediately before archival
-+
-+Additional checkpoints MAY be created when operationally beneficial.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Checkpoint Granularity
-+
-+Checkpoint frequency SHALL balance:
-+
-+- Recovery precision
-+- Storage efficiency
-+- Execution overhead
-+- Operational requirements
-+
-+Checkpoint intervals SHOULD remain configurable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Captured State
-+
-+Each checkpoint SHALL capture:
-+
-+- Current workflow state
-+- Completed work
-+- Outstanding work
-+- Active dependencies
-+- Execution parameters
-+- Repository context
-+
-+Captured state SHALL be sufficient for deterministic recovery.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Provenance
-+
-+Every checkpoint SHALL extend the workflow provenance chain.
-+
-+Checkpoint provenance SHALL identify:
-+
-+- Creation event
-+- Responsible service
-+- Execution context
-+- Repository state
-+
-+No checkpoint SHALL break provenance continuity.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Validation
-+
-+Checkpoint creation SHALL verify:
-+
-+- Workflow integrity
-+- Execution consistency
-+- Repository consistency
-+- State validity
-+- Provenance completeness
-+
-+Invalid checkpoints SHALL NOT be persisted.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Recovery
-+
-+Recovery SHALL:
-+
-+- Locate the most recent valid checkpoint.
-+- Validate checkpoint integrity.
-+- Restore execution context.
-+- Resume from the next executable step.
-+- Record recovery events.
-+
-+Recovery SHALL preserve execution history.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Checkpoint Integrity
-+
-+Checkpoint records SHALL remain immutable after creation.
-+
-+Integrity verification SHOULD include:
-+
-+- Identifier validation
-+- Schema validation
-+- Provenance verification
-+- Repository consistency checks
-+- State consistency checks
-+
-+Compromised checkpoints SHALL be rejected.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Retention
-+
-+Checkpoint retention SHALL follow repository governance policies.
-+
-+Retention MAY vary according to:
-+
-+- Workflow type
-+- Repository classification
-+- Operational requirements
-+- Regulatory requirements
-+
-+Expired checkpoints SHALL be archived or removed according to approved retention schedules.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Monitoring
-+
-+Checkpoint services SHOULD expose:
-+
-+- Total checkpoints
-+- Checkpoint frequency
-+- Recovery success rate
-+- Checkpoint validation failures
-+- Average recovery time
-+- Storage utilization
-+
-+Monitoring SHALL support operational optimization.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Repository Integration
-+
-+Checkpointing integrates with:
-+
-+- Workflow Execution
-+- Workflow State Machine
-+- Workflow Context
-+- Event Processing
-+- Provenance
-+- Lifecycle Management
-+- Repository Indexes
-+- Observability
-+
-+Checkpoint creation SHALL preserve repository consistency.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Philosophy
-+
-+Workflow execution is a continuous process, but reliable execution depends upon preserving meaningful milestones.
-+
-+Checkpoints transform transient execution into durable institutional knowledge, enabling workflows to recover predictably while preserving determinism, provenance, and complete execution history.
-+
-+---
-+
-+End of Specification.
-\ No newline at end of file
