-+# Workflow Definition Schema
-+
-+**Version:** 0.1
-+**Status:** Draft
-+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Purpose
-+
-+The Workflow Definition Schema specifies the canonical structure used to define executable workflows within the Repository Ingestion Framework.
-+
-+The Workflow Orchestration Specification defines workflow behavior.
-+
-+This schema defines how workflows are represented.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Principles
-+
-+- Workflow definitions are declarative.
-+- Definitions are versioned.
-+- Definitions are deterministic.
-+- Definitions are independently validatable.
-+- Definitions remain implementation-neutral.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Canonical Workflow Schema
-+
-+Every workflow definition SHALL contain the following fields.
-+
-+| Field | Required | Description |
-+|--------|----------|-------------|
-+| Workflow ID | Yes | Unique workflow identifier |
-+| Workflow Name | Yes | Human-readable name |
-+| Workflow Version | Yes | Workflow definition version |
-+| Workflow Type | Yes | Classification of workflow |
-+| Status | Yes | Current definition status |
-+| Description | Yes | Purpose of the workflow |
-+| Trigger | Yes | Event or condition initiating execution |
-+| Steps | Yes | Ordered workflow steps |
-+| Dependencies | No | Workflow-level dependencies |
-+| Inputs | Yes | Required inputs |
-+| Outputs | Yes | Produced outputs |
-+| Retry Policy | No | Retry configuration |
-+| Timeout | No | Maximum execution duration |
-+| Owner | Yes | Responsible subsystem |
-+| Related Specifications | No | Governing specifications |
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Workflow Identifier
-+
-+Workflow IDs SHALL:
-+
-+- Be globally unique.
-+- Remain stable.
-+- Never be reused.
-+- Be machine-readable.
-+
-+Example:
-+
-+WF-INGEST-001
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Workflow Types
-+
-+Supported workflow types include:
-+
-+- Ingestion
-+- Validation
-+- Mapping
-+- Governance
-+- Publication
-+- Maintenance
-+- Automation
-+
-+Additional workflow types MAY be defined through governance.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Definition Status
-+
-+Workflow definitions SHALL exist in one of the following states:
-+
-+- Draft
-+- Review
-+- Approved
-+- Active
-+- Deprecated
-+- Archived
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Trigger Definition
-+
-+Every workflow SHALL declare at least one trigger.
-+
-+Examples include:
-+
-+- Event received
-+- Scheduled execution
-+- Manual invocation
-+- Repository state transition
-+- Administrative action
-+
-+Triggers SHALL be explicit.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Workflow Steps
-+
-+Each workflow SHALL define one or more execution steps.
-+
-+Each step SHALL include:
-+
-+- Step ID
-+- Step Name
-+- Responsible Service
-+- Expected Input
-+- Expected Output
-+
-+Step ordering SHALL be deterministic.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Dependencies
-+
-+Dependencies MAY define:
-+
-+- Required predecessor steps
-+- External workflow dependencies
-+- Required services
-+- Required repository state
-+
-+Circular dependencies SHALL NOT be permitted.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Inputs
-+
-+Workflow inputs SHALL specify:
-+
-+- Input Name
-+- Input Type
-+- Required or Optional
-+- Validation Rules
-+
-+Inputs SHALL conform to approved schemas.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Outputs
-+
-+Workflow outputs SHALL specify:
-+
-+- Output Name
-+- Output Type
-+- Destination
-+- Validation Rules
-+
-+Outputs SHALL preserve provenance.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Retry Policy
-+
-+Retry configuration MAY define:
-+
-+- Maximum retries
-+- Retry interval
-+- Retry conditions
-+- Failure threshold
-+
-+Retry policies SHALL avoid infinite execution loops.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Timeout
-+
-+Workflows MAY define a maximum execution duration.
-+
-+Timeout behavior SHALL specify:
-+
-+- Timeout threshold
-+- Cancellation policy
-+- Recovery behavior
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Validation Rules
-+
-+Workflow definitions SHALL validate:
-+
-+- Required fields
-+- Unique Step IDs
-+- Dependency integrity
-+- Trigger validity
-+- Input and output schemas
-+- Version compatibility
-+
-+Invalid workflow definitions SHALL NOT be activated.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Repository Integration
-+
-+Workflow definitions integrate with:
-+
-+- Workflow Orchestration
-+- Event Model
-+- Event Routing
-+- Event Processing
-+- Service Contracts
-+- Validation
-+- Observability
-+
-+Workflow definitions SHALL remain consistent with governing specifications.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Philosophy
-+
-+A workflow definition describes *what* will occur.
-+
-+The orchestration engine determines *when* it occurs.
-+
-+The participating services determine *how* it occurs.
-+
-+Separating these responsibilities preserves modularity, governance, and long-term maintainability.
-+
-+---
-+
-+End of Specification.
-\ No newline at end of file
