-+# Workflow Execution Specification
-+
-+**Version:** 0.1
-+**Status:** Draft
-+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Purpose
-+
-+The Workflow Execution Specification defines the canonical rules governing the execution of workflows within the Repository Ingestion Framework.
-+
-+The Workflow Definition Schema describes what a workflow is.
-+
-+The Workflow Orchestration Specification coordinates workflows.
-+
-+This specification defines how workflows execute.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Principles
-+
-+- Execution is deterministic.
-+- Execution is observable.
-+- Execution preserves provenance.
-+- Execution is resumable.
-+- Execution is reproducible.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Scope
-+
-+This specification applies to:
-+
-+- Workflow execution engines
-+- Workflow coordinators
-+- Service execution
-+- Workflow state management
-+- Recovery mechanisms
-+- Execution reporting
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Execution Lifecycle
-+
-+Every workflow execution SHALL progress through the following stages:
-+
-+1. Initialize
-+2. Validate
-+3. Prepare
-+4. Execute
-+5. Verify
-+6. Complete
-+7. Archive
-+
-+Alternative termination paths include:
-+
-+- Failed
-+- Cancelled
-+- Suspended
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Initialization
-+
-+Initialization SHALL:
-+
-+- Generate an Execution ID.
-+- Associate a Workflow ID.
-+- Associate a Correlation ID.
-+- Initialize execution context.
-+- Record start time.
-+
-+Initialization SHALL generate an execution event.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Validation
-+
-+Prior to execution, the workflow engine SHALL verify:
-+
-+- Workflow definition validity.
-+- Required inputs.
-+- Dependency satisfaction.
-+- Service availability.
-+- Schema compatibility.
-+
-+Validation failures SHALL prevent execution.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Preparation
-+
-+Preparation MAY include:
-+
-+- Resource allocation.
-+- Context construction.
-+- Input normalization.
-+- Dependency resolution.
-+
-+Preparation SHALL NOT modify repository artifacts.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Execution
-+
-+Workflow steps SHALL execute according to the approved workflow definition.
-+
-+Execution SHALL:
-+
-+- Preserve workflow order.
-+- Honor dependencies.
-+- Record execution history.
-+- Publish execution events.
-+
-+Execution SHALL remain deterministic.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Step Execution
-+
-+Each workflow step SHALL record:
-+
-+- Step ID
-+- Responsible Service
-+- Start Time
-+- End Time
-+- Duration
-+- Result
-+- Diagnostics
-+
-+Every step SHALL be independently observable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Verification
-+
-+Following execution, the workflow engine SHALL verify:
-+
-+- Required outputs produced.
-+- Validation completed.
-+- Repository consistency preserved.
-+- Expected events published.
-+
-+Verification SHALL generate an execution report.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Completion
-+
-+Successful execution SHALL:
-+
-+- Record completion time.
-+- Publish completion event.
-+- Persist execution history.
-+- Archive execution metadata.
-+
-+Completion SHALL preserve provenance.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Suspension
-+
-+Workflows MAY be suspended when:
-+
-+- Dependencies are unavailable.
-+- External approval is required.
-+- Resources are temporarily unavailable.
-+
-+Suspended workflows SHALL preserve execution context.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Failure Handling
-+
-+Failures SHALL:
-+
-+- Preserve completed work.
-+- Record diagnostics.
-+- Preserve execution history.
-+- Publish failure events.
-+- Support recovery where appropriate.
-+
-+Failures SHALL NOT corrupt repository state.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Recovery
-+
-+Recoverable workflows MAY resume execution from the most recent completed step.
-+
-+Recovery SHALL preserve:
-+
-+- Workflow ID
-+- Execution ID
-+- Correlation ID
-+- Provenance
-+- Execution history
-+
-+Recovery events SHALL be recorded.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Cancellation
-+
-+Cancellation SHALL:
-+
-+- Record reason.
-+- Record initiator.
-+- Preserve completed outputs.
-+- Publish cancellation events.
-+
-+Cancelled workflows SHALL remain historically visible.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Execution Reports
-+
-+Each workflow execution SHALL produce a report containing:
-+
-+- Execution ID
-+- Workflow ID
-+- Start Time
-+- End Time
-+- Total Duration
-+- Final Status
-+- Steps Executed
-+- Failures
-+- Warnings
-+- Related Events
-+
-+Execution reports SHALL support auditing.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Monitoring
-+
-+Workflow execution SHOULD expose:
-+
-+- Active executions
-+- Completed executions
-+- Failed executions
-+- Average execution duration
-+- Retry counts
-+- Recovery counts
-+- Resource utilization
-+
-+Operational metrics SHALL support continuous improvement.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Validation Rules
-+
-+Workflow execution SHALL validate:
-+
-+- Execution state transitions.
-+- Step ordering.
-+- Dependency completion.
-+- Output consistency.
-+- Event publication.
-+- Provenance preservation.
-+
-+Invalid executions SHALL be rejected or terminated safely.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Repository Integration
-+
-+Workflow execution integrates with:
-+
-+- Workflow Definition Schema
-+- Workflow Orchestration
-+- Event Model
-+- Event Processing
-+- Lifecycle Management
-+- Validation
-+- Observability
-+- Repository Indexes
-+
-+Execution SHALL preserve repository integrity at every stage.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Philosophy
-+
-+Workflow definitions describe intent.
-+
-+Workflow orchestration coordinates execution.
-+
-+Workflow execution transforms intent into observable, governed activity.
-+
-+Reliable execution ensures that every repository operation becomes reproducible institutional history rather than transient computation.
-+
-+---
-+
-+End of Specification.
-\ No newline at end of file
