-+# Workflow Recovery Specification
-+
-+**Version:** 0.1  
-+**Status:** Draft  
-+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Purpose
-+
-+The Workflow Recovery Specification defines the canonical mechanisms for restoring interrupted workflows to a valid execution state while preserving determinism, provenance, and repository integrity.
-+
-+Recovery enables workflows to resume safely after failures, interruptions, or planned suspensions without compromising institutional history.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Principles
-+
-+- Recovery is deterministic.
-+- Recovery preserves provenance.
-+- Recovery is repeatable.
-+- Recovery is observable.
-+- Recovery never compromises repository integrity.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Scope
-+
-+This specification applies to:
-+
-+- Workflow Execution
-+- Workflow Orchestration
-+- Checkpoint Management
-+- Failure Handling
-+- Service Recovery
-+- Repository Operations
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Recovery Definition
-+
-+Recovery is the controlled restoration of a workflow from a previously validated execution state.
-+
-+Recovery SHALL resume execution without repeating successfully completed work unless explicitly required by policy.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Recovery Objectives
-+
-+Recovery SHALL:
-+
-+- Restore execution context.
-+- Preserve completed work.
-+- Maintain provenance continuity.
-+- Resume deterministic execution.
-+- Produce an auditable recovery history.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Recovery Triggers
-+
-+Recovery MAY be initiated by:
-+
-+- Service failure
-+- Infrastructure failure
-+- System restart
-+- Workflow timeout
-+- Administrative request
-+- Planned maintenance
-+- External dependency restoration
-+
-+All recovery triggers SHALL be recorded.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Recovery Prerequisites
-+
-+Recovery SHALL require:
-+
-+- Valid Workflow Definition
-+- Valid Workflow Context
-+- Valid Execution Record
-+- Valid Checkpoint
-+- Valid Provenance Chain
-+
-+Recovery SHALL NOT begin if prerequisite validation fails.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Recovery Sources
-+
-+Recovery SHALL restore execution using:
-+
-+- Latest valid checkpoint
-+- Execution history
-+- Workflow context
-+- Repository metadata
-+- Provenance records
-+
-+Recovery SHALL use the most recent validated checkpoint available.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Recovery Lifecycle
-+
-+A recovery operation SHALL proceed through the following stages:
-+
-+1. Detect Interruption
-+2. Validate Recovery Eligibility
-+3. Locate Recovery Point
-+4. Restore Execution Context
-+5. Verify Repository Consistency
-+6. Resume Execution
-+7. Record Recovery Completion
-+
-+Each stage SHALL generate appropriate execution events.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Recovery Eligibility
-+
-+A workflow SHALL be eligible for recovery only if:
-+
-+- Execution history is complete.
-+- Required checkpoints exist.
-+- Repository integrity is verified.
-+- Dependencies remain satisfiable.
-+- Workflow definition remains compatible.
-+
-+Ineligible workflows SHALL transition to the Failed state.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Context Restoration
-+
-+Recovery SHALL restore:
-+
-+- Workflow ID
-+- Execution ID
-+- Correlation ID
-+- Workflow State
-+- Execution Parameters
-+- Repository Context
-+- Provenance References
-+
-+Context restoration SHALL be validated before execution resumes.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Repository Validation
-+
-+Prior to recovery, the repository SHALL verify:
-+
-+- Artifact integrity
-+- Asset integrity
-+- Reference integrity
-+- Repository indexes
-+- Schema compatibility
-+- Configuration consistency
-+
-+Recovery SHALL NOT continue if repository validation fails.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Step Resumption
-+
-+Recovery SHALL resume from the next executable workflow step following the latest validated checkpoint.
-+
-+Previously completed steps SHALL NOT be re-executed unless:
-+
-+- The workflow explicitly requires replay.
-+- Repository policy requires revalidation.
-+- Recovery validation determines replay is necessary.
-+
-+All replayed steps SHALL be recorded.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Recovery Events
-+
-+Recovery SHALL publish events including:
-+
-+- Recovery Initiated
-+- Recovery Validated
-+- Context Restored
-+- Recovery Resumed
-+- Recovery Completed
-+- Recovery Failed
-+
-+Events SHALL integrate with the canonical Event Model.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Recovery Failure
-+
-+Recovery MAY fail due to:
-+
-+- Missing checkpoints
-+- Invalid execution history
-+- Repository corruption
-+- Schema incompatibility
-+- Dependency failure
-+- Configuration inconsistency
-+
-+Recovery failures SHALL preserve diagnostic information.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Audit Requirements
-+
-+Every recovery operation SHALL record:
-+
-+- Recovery ID
-+- Workflow ID
-+- Execution ID
-+- Recovery trigger
-+- Recovery timestamp
-+- Recovery operator or service
-+- Recovery outcome
-+- Diagnostic information
-+
-+Recovery records SHALL be immutable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Monitoring
-+
-+Recovery services SHOULD expose:
-+
-+- Recovery attempts
-+- Successful recoveries
-+- Failed recoveries
-+- Average recovery duration
-+- Recovery success rate
-+- Recovery replay count
-+- Recovery validation failures
-+
-+Operational metrics SHALL support continuous improvement.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Validation
-+
-+Recovery SHALL validate:
-+
-+- Checkpoint integrity
-+- Workflow compatibility
-+- Repository consistency
-+- Provenance continuity
-+- Execution state
-+- Dependency availability
-+
-+Validation SHALL complete before execution resumes.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Repository Integration
-+
-+Workflow Recovery integrates with:
-+
-+- Workflow Checkpointing
-+- Workflow Context
-+- Workflow Execution
-+- Workflow State Machine
-+- Event Processing
-+- Provenance
-+- Lifecycle Management
-+- Repository Indexes
-+- Observability
-+
-+Recovery SHALL preserve repository integrity throughout its lifecycle.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Philosophy
-+
-+Interruptions are inevitable in long-running systems.
-+
-+Recovery transforms interruption into controlled continuation by ensuring that execution resumes from verified institutional knowledge rather than uncertain transient state.
-+
-+A recovered workflow is not a new workflow—it is the faithful continuation of an existing execution, preserving its identity, provenance, and complete operational history.
-+
-+---
-+
-+End of Specification.
-\ No newline at end of file
