-+# Workflow Service Composition Specification
-+
-+**Version:** 0.1  
-+**Status:** Draft  
-+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Purpose
-+
-+The Workflow Service Composition Specification defines the canonical model for combining independent workflow services into larger coordinated execution capabilities.
-+
-+Service Composition enables complex repository operations to be assembled from reusable, governed, and independently managed services while preserving determinism, provenance, and architectural integrity.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Principles
-+
-+- Composition is declarative.
-+- Services remain independently deployable.
-+- Composition is deterministic.
-+- Composition preserves provenance.
-+- Composition is independently testable.
-+- Composition is governed.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Scope
-+
-+This specification applies to:
-+
-+- Workflow Orchestration
-+- Workflow Execution
-+- Parser Services
-+- Validation Services
-+- Mapping Services
-+- Publication Services
-+- Governance Services
-+- Utility Services
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Composition Definition
-+
-+Service Composition is the controlled assembly of multiple services into a coordinated execution sequence that accomplishes a higher-level workflow objective.
-+
-+Composition defines cooperation rather than implementation.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Composition Objectives
-+
-+Service Composition SHALL provide:
-+
-+- Reusable workflow construction
-+- Modular execution
-+- Independent service evolution
-+- Deterministic execution
-+- Architectural scalability
-+- Institutional consistency
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Canonical Composition Schema
-+
-+Every service composition SHALL contain:
-+
-+| Field | Required | Description |
-+|--------|----------|-------------|
-+| Composition ID | Yes | Unique composition identifier |
-+| Composition Name | Yes | Human-readable name |
-+| Composition Version | Yes | Semantic version |
-+| Description | Yes | Functional description |
-+| Participating Services | Yes | Ordered list of services |
-+| Service Roles | Yes | Responsibilities within the composition |
-+| Execution Order | Yes | Execution sequencing rules |
-+| Coordination Rules | Yes | Service interaction rules |
-+| Failure Policy | Yes | Failure handling behavior |
-+| Provenance ID | Yes | Provenance reference |
-+| Metadata | No | Supplemental composition information |
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Composition Identifier
-+
-+Composition identifiers SHALL:
-+
-+- Be globally unique.
-+- Remain immutable.
-+- Never be reused.
-+- Support repository indexing.
-+
-+Example:
-+
-+COMP-INGEST-001
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Participating Services
-+
-+Each composition SHALL explicitly identify:
-+
-+- Service ID
-+- Service Version
-+- Service Capability
-+- Service Contract
-+- Service Role
-+
-+Only Active services SHALL participate in production compositions.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Service Roles
-+
-+Service roles MAY include:
-+
-+- Coordinator
-+- Producer
-+- Consumer
-+- Validator
-+- Transformer
-+- Publisher
-+- Observer
-+- Utility
-+
-+Additional roles MAY be defined through governance.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Execution Order
-+
-+Execution order SHALL specify:
-+
-+- Sequential execution
-+- Parallel execution
-+- Conditional execution
-+- Iterative execution
-+- Completion dependencies
-+
-+Execution ordering SHALL remain deterministic.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Coordination Rules
-+
-+Coordination SHALL define:
-+
-+- Data exchange
-+- Context propagation
-+- Event publication
-+- State synchronization
-+- Dependency resolution
-+
-+Coordination SHALL preserve service independence.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Composition Boundaries
-+
-+A composition SHALL define:
-+
-+- Entry point
-+- Exit conditions
-+- Input boundaries
-+- Output boundaries
-+- Failure boundaries
-+
-+Boundaries SHALL be explicit and verifiable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Failure Policy
-+
-+Every composition SHALL define behavior for:
-+
-+- Service failure
-+- Dependency failure
-+- Timeout
-+- Validation failure
-+- Cancellation
-+- Recovery
-+
-+Failure handling SHALL preserve repository integrity.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Version Compatibility
-+
-+Composition SHALL verify compatibility across:
-+
-+- Participating services
-+- Service contracts
-+- Workflow definitions
-+- Event schemas
-+- Repository schemas
-+- API versions
-+
-+Incompatible compositions SHALL NOT execute.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Composition Lifecycle
-+
-+Compositions SHALL progress through:
-+
-+Draft
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Review
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Approved
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Registered
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Active
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Deprecated
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Archived
-+
-+Lifecycle SHALL be governed by repository policy.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Composition Validation
-+
-+Every composition SHALL validate:
-+
-+- Service availability
-+- Capability compatibility
-+- Contract compatibility
-+- Dependency integrity
-+- Execution ordering
-+- Governance approval
-+
-+Validation SHALL precede activation.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Composition Events
-+
-+Composition operations SHALL publish events including:
-+
-+- Composition Created
-+- Composition Validated
-+- Composition Activated
-+- Composition Executed
-+- Composition Completed
-+- Composition Failed
-+- Composition Deprecated
-+- Composition Archived
-+
-+Events SHALL conform to the Event Model Specification.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Audit Requirements
-+
-+Composition management SHALL record:
-+
-+- Composition ID
-+- Version
-+- Participating services
-+- Validation results
-+- Governance approvals
-+- Lifecycle transitions
-+- Provenance references
-+
-+Audit records SHALL remain immutable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Monitoring
-+
-+Composition services SHOULD expose:
-+
-+- Active compositions
-+- Composition executions
-+- Execution duration
-+- Failure rates
-+- Dependency violations
-+- Service utilization
-+- Composition health
-+
-+Monitoring SHALL support operational governance.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Repository Integration
-+
-+Workflow Service Composition integrates with:
-+
-+- Workflow Orchestration
-+- Workflow Execution
-+- Workflow Service Registry
-+- Workflow Service Discovery
-+- Workflow Service Contracts
-+- Workflow Service Capabilities
-+- Workflow Service Dependencies
-+- Event Processing
-+- Repository Indexes
-+- Observability
-+
-+Composition SHALL preserve repository integrity and institutional accountability.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Philosophy
-+
-+Individual services provide focused capabilities.
-+
-+Service composition transforms those capabilities into cohesive institutional processes.
-+
-+By treating compositions as governed architectural assets rather than ad hoc implementations, the Repository Ingestion Framework enables scalable, reusable, and maintainable workflow architectures while preserving modularity, determinism, and long-term institutional knowledge.
-+
-+---
-+
-+End of Specification.
-\ No newline at end of file
