-+# Workflow Service Contract Specification
-+
-+**Version:** 0.1  
-+**Status:** Draft  
-+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Purpose
-+
-+The Workflow Service Contract Specification defines the canonical contracts between workflow orchestration and participating services.
-+
-+Service contracts establish standardized expectations for communication, execution behavior, inputs, outputs, error handling, and lifecycle management.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Principles
-+
-+- Contracts are explicit.
-+- Contracts are versioned.
-+- Contracts are deterministic.
-+- Contracts are implementation-independent.
-+- Contracts preserve provenance.
-+- Contracts support interoperability.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Scope
-+
-+This specification applies to:
-+
-+- Workflow Services
-+- Orchestration Services
-+- Validation Services
-+- Parser Services
-+- Asset Services
-+- Mapping Services
-+- Governance Services
-+- Publication Services
-+- Administrative Services
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Service Contract Definition
-+
-+A service contract defines the formal agreement between a service provider and its consumers.
-+
-+A contract SHALL specify:
-+
-+- Service capabilities
-+- Inputs
-+- Outputs
-+- Execution expectations
-+- Failure behavior
-+- Version compatibility
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Canonical Service Contract Schema
-+
-+Every service contract SHALL contain:
-+
-+| Field | Required | Description |
-+|--------|----------|-------------|
-+| Contract ID | Yes | Unique contract identifier |
-+| Contract Name | Yes | Human-readable name |
-+| Contract Version | Yes | Semantic version identifier |
-+| Service Name | Yes | Responsible service |
-+| Service Type | Yes | Functional classification |
-+| Status | Yes | Contract lifecycle state |
-+| Inputs | Yes | Accepted inputs |
-+| Outputs | Yes | Produced outputs |
-+| Preconditions | No | Required execution conditions |
-+| Postconditions | No | Expected completion conditions |
-+| Error Model | Yes | Defined error responses |
-+| Related Specifications | No | Governing specifications |
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Contract Identifier
-+
-+Contract IDs SHALL:
-+
-+- Be globally unique.
-+- Never be reused.
-+- Remain immutable.
-+- Support repository indexing.
-+
-+Example:
-+
-+SC-VALIDATION-001
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Service Classification
-+
-+Service types MAY include:
-+
-+- Parser
-+- Validator
-+- Mapper
-+- Publisher
-+- Governance
-+- Repository
-+- Event
-+- Utility
-+- Administrative
-+
-+Additional classifications MAY be introduced through governance.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Contract Lifecycle
-+
-+Service contracts SHALL progress through:
-+
-+Draft
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Review
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Approved
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Active
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Deprecated
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Archived
-+
-+Lifecycle transitions SHALL follow repository governance policy.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Input Contract
-+
-+Every contract SHALL define:
-+
-+- Input name
-+- Input type
-+- Required or optional
-+- Validation rules
-+- Schema reference
-+
-+Inputs SHALL conform to approved schemas.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Output Contract
-+
-+Every contract SHALL define:
-+
-+- Output name
-+- Output type
-+- Validation rules
-+- Schema reference
-+- Provenance requirements
-+
-+Outputs SHALL preserve repository integrity.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Preconditions
-+
-+Preconditions MAY specify:
-+
-+- Required workflow state
-+- Required permissions
-+- Repository readiness
-+- Dependency completion
-+- Resource availability
-+
-+Services SHALL validate preconditions before execution.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Postconditions
-+
-+Postconditions MAY specify:
-+
-+- Expected outputs
-+- Repository updates
-+- Published events
-+- Validation completion
-+- State transitions
-+
-+Postconditions SHALL be verifiable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Error Model
-+
-+Every contract SHALL define structured error responses including:
-+
-+- Error code
-+- Error category
-+- Error description
-+- Recovery recommendation
-+- Severity
-+- Correlation ID
-+
-+Errors SHALL remain implementation-neutral.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Execution Requirements
-+
-+Services SHALL:
-+
-+- Accept canonical workflow context.
-+- Preserve provenance.
-+- Produce deterministic results.
-+- Publish required events.
-+- Return structured responses.
-+
-+Execution SHALL comply with approved workflow definitions.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Compatibility
-+
-+Service contracts SHALL identify compatibility with:
-+
-+- Workflow versions
-+- Schema versions
-+- Event versions
-+- Repository versions
-+- API versions
-+
-+Compatibility SHALL be explicitly documented.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Versioning
-+
-+Service contracts SHALL follow Semantic Versioning.
-+
-+Major versions indicate breaking changes.
-+
-+Minor versions introduce compatible capabilities.
-+
-+Patch versions provide non-breaking corrections.
-+
-+Historical versions SHALL remain identifiable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Validation
-+
-+Service contracts SHALL validate:
-+
-+- Schema compliance
-+- Input definitions
-+- Output definitions
-+- Identifier uniqueness
-+- Version consistency
-+- Dependency compatibility
-+
-+Invalid contracts SHALL NOT become Active.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Monitoring
-+
-+Contract management SHOULD expose:
-+
-+- Active contracts
-+- Deprecated contracts
-+- Contract validation failures
-+- Version distribution
-+- Compatibility issues
-+- Service adoption
-+
-+Monitoring SHALL support governance and operational planning.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Repository Integration
-+
-+Workflow Service Contracts integrate with:
-+
-+- Workflow Definition Schema
-+- Workflow Execution
-+- Workflow API
-+- Workflow Governance
-+- Schema Governance
-+- Event Processing
-+- Repository Indexes
-+- Observability
-+
-+Service contracts SHALL preserve repository consistency and institutional accountability.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Philosophy
-+
-+Workflows coordinate services, but service contracts establish trust between them.
-+
-+By defining stable, versioned, and implementation-independent agreements, the Repository Ingestion Framework enables independent evolution of services while ensuring that workflow execution remains deterministic, interoperable, and governed across the lifetime of the repository.
-+
-+---
-+
-+End of Specification.
-\ No newline at end of file
