-+# Workflow Service Coordination Specification
-+
-+**Version:** 0.1  
-+**Status:** Draft  
-+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Purpose
-+
-+The Workflow Service Coordination Specification defines the canonical architecture for coordinating interactions among workflow services participating in the Repository Ingestion Framework.
-+
-+Service Coordination establishes deterministic collaboration between independently governed services while preserving execution integrity, repository consistency, provenance, and institutional accountability.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Principles
-+
-+- Coordination is explicit.
-+- Coordination is deterministic.
-+- Coordination preserves service autonomy.
-+- Coordination is event-aware.
-+- Coordination is observable.
-+- Coordination is fully auditable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Scope
-+
-+This specification applies to:
-+
-+- Workflow Services
-+- Parser Services
-+- Validation Services
-+- Mapping Services
-+- Repository Services
-+- Publication Services
-+- Governance Services
-+- Utility Services
-+- Administrative Services
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Coordination Definition
-+
-+Service Coordination is the governed management of communication, sequencing, synchronization, and information exchange among multiple services participating in a workflow execution.
-+
-+Coordination SHALL govern collaboration without altering individual service responsibilities.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Coordination Objectives
-+
-+Service coordination SHALL provide:
-+
-+- Deterministic collaboration
-+- Consistent execution sequencing
-+- Reliable state synchronization
-+- Controlled communication
-+- Failure isolation
-+- Governance visibility
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Canonical Coordination Schema
-+
-+Every coordination policy SHALL contain:
-+
-+| Field | Required | Description |
-+|--------|----------|-------------|
-+| Coordination ID | Yes | Unique coordination identifier |
-+| Coordination Name | Yes | Human-readable name |
-+| Coordination Version | Yes | Semantic version |
-+| Participating Services | Yes | Coordinated service set |
-+| Coordination Model | Yes | Collaboration methodology |
-+| Communication Rules | Yes | Service interaction rules |
-+| Synchronization Policy | Yes | State synchronization requirements |
-+| Error Coordination Policy | Yes | Failure coordination rules |
-+| Provenance ID | Yes | Provenance reference |
-+| Metadata | No | Supplemental coordination information |
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Coordination Models
-+
-+Supported coordination models include:
-+
-+- Sequential Coordination
-+- Parallel Coordination
-+- Event-Driven Coordination
-+- Orchestrated Coordination
-+- Choreographed Coordination
-+- Dependency-Driven Coordination
-+- Hybrid Coordination
-+
-+Additional coordination models MAY be introduced through governance.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Participating Services
-+
-+Every coordination policy SHALL identify:
-+
-+- Service ID
-+- Service Version
-+- Declared Capability
-+- Lifecycle State
-+- Assigned Coordination Role
-+
-+Only Active and Healthy services SHALL participate in production coordination.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Coordination Roles
-+
-+Services MAY perform one or more coordination roles including:
-+
-+- Coordinator
-+- Participant
-+- Observer
-+- Validator
-+- Publisher
-+- Consumer
-+- Synchronizer
-+- Recovery Coordinator
-+
-+Roles SHALL be explicitly declared.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Communication Rules
-+
-+Coordination SHALL define:
-+
-+- Permitted communication paths
-+- Request-response interactions
-+- Event publication rules
-+- Message ordering
-+- Timeout behavior
-+- Communication constraints
-+
-+Communication SHALL remain deterministic.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Synchronization Policy
-+
-+Synchronization SHALL govern:
-+
-+- Shared execution state
-+- Context propagation
-+- Checkpoint synchronization
-+- Completion synchronization
-+- Repository consistency
-+- Correlation identifiers
-+
-+Synchronization SHALL preserve workflow integrity.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Execution Coordination
-+
-+Coordinated execution SHALL specify:
-+
-+- Start conditions
-+- Completion conditions
-+- Dependency ordering
-+- Parallel execution limits
-+- Synchronization barriers
-+- Termination conditions
-+
-+Execution SHALL remain reproducible.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Context Propagation
-+
-+Execution context SHALL propagate consistently across participating services.
-+
-+Context SHALL include:
-+
-+- Workflow ID
-+- Execution ID
-+- Correlation ID
-+- Repository Context
-+- Provenance ID
-+- Execution Parameters
-+- Security Context
-+
-+Context integrity SHALL be maintained throughout execution.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# State Coordination
-+
-+Coordinated services SHALL maintain consistent operational state.
-+
-+State transitions SHALL:
-+
-+- Follow approved state machines
-+- Preserve ordering
-+- Prevent conflicts
-+- Support recovery
-+- Remain observable
-+
-+Conflicting state transitions SHALL be rejected.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Error Coordination
-+
-+Coordination policies SHALL define behavior for:
-+
-+- Participant failure
-+- Coordinator failure
-+- Communication failure
-+- Synchronization timeout
-+- Dependency failure
-+- Validation failure
-+
-+Error handling SHALL preserve repository integrity.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Recovery Coordination
-+
-+Recovery SHALL coordinate:
-+
-+- Service restoration
-+- Checkpoint restoration
-+- Dependency revalidation
-+- Context reconstruction
-+- Execution replay
-+- Completion verification
-+
-+Recovery SHALL integrate with the Workflow Recovery Specification.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Coordination Constraints
-+
-+Coordination SHALL enforce:
-+
-+- Contract compatibility
-+- Capability compatibility
-+- Version compatibility
-+- Dependency requirements
-+- Repository boundaries
-+- Governance policies
-+
-+Constraint violations SHALL prevent execution.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Validation
-+
-+Coordination validation SHALL verify:
-+
-+- Service registration
-+- Coordination policy integrity
-+- Communication consistency
-+- Synchronization rules
-+- Dependency readiness
-+- Repository connectivity
-+
-+Invalid coordination policies SHALL NOT become Active.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Coordination Events
-+
-+Coordination SHALL publish events including:
-+
-+- Coordination Created
-+- Coordination Activated
-+- Coordination Started
-+- Service Joined Coordination
-+- Synchronization Achieved
-+- Coordination Interrupted
-+- Coordination Resumed
-+- Coordination Completed
-+- Coordination Failed
-+- Coordination Archived
-+
-+Events SHALL conform to the Event Model Specification.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Audit Requirements
-+
-+Coordination activities SHALL record:
-+
-+- Coordination ID
-+- Participating services
-+- Coordination model
-+- Synchronization events
-+- Validation results
-+- Execution timestamps
-+- Responsible component
-+- Provenance reference
-+
-+Audit records SHALL remain immutable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Monitoring
-+
-+Coordination services SHOULD expose:
-+
-+- Active coordinations
-+- Coordinated services
-+- Synchronization latency
-+- Communication failures
-+- Coordination duration
-+- Recovery activities
-+- Policy violations
-+- Coordination success rate
-+
-+Monitoring SHALL support operational governance and continuous improvement.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Security
-+
-+Coordination SHALL:
-+
-+- Authenticate participating services.
-+- Authorize coordination operations.
-+- Protect execution context.
-+- Validate communication integrity.
-+- Record privileged coordination actions.
-+
-+Unauthorized participation SHALL be prohibited.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Repository Integration
-+
-+Workflow Service Coordination integrates with:
-+
-+- Workflow Orchestration
-+- Workflow Execution
-+- Workflow Service Registry
-+- Workflow Service Discovery
-+- Workflow Service Dependencies
-+- Workflow Service Composition
-+- Workflow Service Scheduling
-+- Workflow Service Health
-+- Workflow Recovery
-+- Event Processing
-+- Observability
-+- Repository Indexes
-+
-+Coordination SHALL preserve repository integrity, deterministic execution, and institutional accountability.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Philosophy
-+
-+Independent services achieve institutional value through disciplined collaboration rather than direct coupling.
-+
-+By treating coordination as a governed architectural capability with explicit policies, synchronization rules, and auditable interactions, the Repository Ingestion Framework enables complex, scalable workflows while preserving modularity, determinism, provenance, and long-term maintainability.
-+
-+---
-+
-+End of Specification.
-\ No newline at end of file
