-+# Workflow Service Lifecycle Specification
-+
-+**Version:** 0.1  
-+**Status:** Draft  
-+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Purpose
-+
-+The Workflow Service Lifecycle Specification defines the canonical lifecycle governing workflow services from initial conception through retirement.
-+
-+The lifecycle establishes a consistent governance model ensuring that services evolve predictably while preserving repository integrity, provenance, compatibility, and institutional knowledge.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Principles
-+
-+- Service lifecycles are explicit.
-+- Lifecycle transitions are governed.
-+- Services preserve historical identity.
-+- Lifecycle actions are auditable.
-+- Service evolution is deterministic.
-+- Historical records remain immutable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Scope
-+
-+This specification applies to:
-+
-+- Workflow Services
-+- Parser Services
-+- Validation Services
-+- Repository Services
-+- Mapping Services
-+- Governance Services
-+- Publication Services
-+- Utility Services
-+- Administrative Services
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Lifecycle Definition
-+
-+A Service Lifecycle defines the sequence of approved states through which a service progresses during its existence within the Repository Ingestion Framework.
-+
-+Every registered service SHALL follow the canonical lifecycle.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Lifecycle Objectives
-+
-+The Service Lifecycle SHALL provide:
-+
-+- Controlled service evolution
-+- Governance enforcement
-+- Compatibility management
-+- Operational stability
-+- Historical traceability
-+- Institutional continuity
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Canonical Lifecycle States
-+
-+Every service SHALL exist in one of the following states:
-+
-+- Proposed
-+- Designed
-+- Implemented
-+- Tested
-+- Reviewed
-+- Approved
-+- Registered
-+- Active
-+- Deprecated
-+- Retired
-+- Archived
-+
-+No service SHALL exist outside the approved lifecycle.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Proposed
-+
-+The Proposed state represents the initial registration of a service concept.
-+
-+Proposal SHALL include:
-+
-+- Purpose
-+- Functional scope
-+- Expected capabilities
-+- Initial ownership
-+- Architectural justification
-+
-+Proposed services SHALL NOT participate in workflows.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Designed
-+
-+Designed indicates that the service architecture has been specified.
-+
-+Design documentation SHOULD include:
-+
-+- Service contracts
-+- Capability definitions
-+- Dependency analysis
-+- Interface specifications
-+- Validation strategy
-+
-+Design SHALL undergo technical review.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Implemented
-+
-+Implemented indicates that service development has been completed.
-+
-+Implementation SHALL:
-+
-+- Conform to approved contracts.
-+- Implement declared capabilities.
-+- Preserve provenance.
-+- Support observability.
-+
-+Implementation SHALL precede testing.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Tested
-+
-+Tested indicates successful completion of required verification activities.
-+
-+Testing SHALL include:
-+
-+- Unit testing
-+- Integration testing
-+- Contract testing
-+- Schema validation
-+- Performance verification
-+
-+Testing results SHALL be recorded.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Reviewed
-+
-+Reviewed indicates successful technical evaluation.
-+
-+Review SHALL verify:
-+
-+- Architectural consistency
-+- Coding standards
-+- Security compliance
-+- Repository compatibility
-+- Documentation completeness
-+
-+Review findings SHALL remain auditable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Approved
-+
-+Approved indicates governance authorization for operational deployment.
-+
-+Approval SHALL verify:
-+
-+- Governance compliance
-+- Compatibility
-+- Documentation
-+- Validation results
-+- Operational readiness
-+
-+Only Approved services MAY become Registered.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Registered
-+
-+Registered indicates inclusion within the authoritative Service Registry.
-+
-+Registration SHALL record:
-+
-+- Service metadata
-+- Version
-+- Supported capabilities
-+- Service contracts
-+- Dependencies
-+- Provenance
-+
-+Registered services MAY participate in discovery.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Active
-+
-+Active services are approved for workflow execution.
-+
-+Active services SHALL:
-+
-+- Support declared capabilities.
-+- Publish required events.
-+- Maintain compatibility.
-+- Preserve observability.
-+- Support audit requirements.
-+
-+Active services SHALL remain continuously monitored.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Deprecated
-+
-+Deprecated services remain operational but are scheduled for replacement.
-+
-+Deprecation SHALL include:
-+
-+- Effective date
-+- Deprecation rationale
-+- Recommended replacement
-+- Migration guidance
-+- Planned retirement date
-+
-+Deprecated services SHALL remain discoverable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Retired
-+
-+Retired services SHALL no longer participate in workflow execution.
-+
-+Retirement SHALL:
-+
-+- Preserve historical records.
-+- Preserve provenance.
-+- Preserve audit history.
-+- Preserve registry metadata.
-+
-+Retired services SHALL remain searchable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Archived
-+
-+Archived services become permanent institutional records.
-+
-+Archived services SHALL:
-+
-+- Remain immutable.
-+- Support auditing.
-+- Support historical reporting.
-+- Preserve complete lifecycle history.
-+
-+Archived services SHALL NOT execute.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Lifecycle Transitions
-+
-+Approved lifecycle transitions include:
-+
-+| From | To |
-+|--------|-----|
-+| Proposed | Designed |
-+| Designed | Implemented |
-+| Implemented | Tested |
-+| Tested | Reviewed |
-+| Reviewed | Approved |
-+| Approved | Registered |
-+| Registered | Active |
-+| Active | Deprecated |
-+| Deprecated | Retired |
-+| Retired | Archived |
-+
-+Transitions not listed SHALL be considered invalid unless explicitly approved through governance.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Transition Validation
-+
-+Every lifecycle transition SHALL verify:
-+
-+- Current lifecycle state
-+- Transition legality
-+- Governance authorization
-+- Compatibility requirements
-+- Documentation completeness
-+- Repository consistency
-+
-+Invalid transitions SHALL be rejected.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Lifecycle Events
-+
-+Lifecycle transitions SHALL publish events including:
-+
-+- Service Proposed
-+- Service Designed
-+- Service Implemented
-+- Service Tested
-+- Service Reviewed
-+- Service Approved
-+- Service Registered
-+- Service Activated
-+- Service Deprecated
-+- Service Retired
-+- Service Archived
-+
-+Events SHALL conform to the Event Model Specification.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Audit Requirements
-+
-+Lifecycle operations SHALL record:
-+
-+- Service ID
-+- Lifecycle state
-+- Transition timestamp
-+- Responsible actor
-+- Approval references
-+- Supporting documentation
-+- Provenance references
-+
-+Audit records SHALL remain immutable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Monitoring
-+
-+Lifecycle management SHOULD expose:
-+
-+- Active services
-+- Deprecated services
-+- Pending approvals
-+- Review backlog
-+- Retirement schedule
-+- Lifecycle transition history
-+- Compliance status
-+
-+Monitoring SHALL support governance and operational planning.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Validation
-+
-+Lifecycle management SHALL validate:
-+
-+- Lifecycle state integrity
-+- Transition legality
-+- Registry consistency
-+- Contract compatibility
-+- Capability declarations
-+- Governance approvals
-+
-+Validation SHALL complete before transition approval.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Repository Integration
-+
-+Workflow Service Lifecycle integrates with:
-+
-+- Workflow Service Registry
-+- Workflow Service Discovery
-+- Workflow Service Contracts
-+- Workflow Service Capabilities
-+- Workflow Governance
-+- Repository Constitution
-+- Lifecycle Management
-+- Repository Indexes
-+- Event Processing
-+- Observability
-+
-+Lifecycle management SHALL preserve repository integrity and institutional accountability.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Philosophy
-+
-+Services are long-lived institutional assets rather than temporary software components.
-+
-+A governed lifecycle ensures that every service progresses through disciplined stages of conception, validation, operation, evolution, and retirement while preserving complete historical accountability.
-+
-+By treating service evolution as an institutional process rather than an implementation detail, the Repository Ingestion Framework enables sustainable growth, architectural consistency, and reproducible operations across the lifetime of the repository.
-+
-+---
-+
-+End of Specification.
-\ No newline at end of file
