-+# Workflow Service Messaging Specification
-+
-+**Version:** 0.1  
-+**Status:** Draft  
-+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Purpose
-+
-+The Workflow Service Messaging Specification defines the canonical architecture for creating, transmitting, validating, routing, and governing messages exchanged between workflow services within the Repository Ingestion Framework.
-+
-+Messaging provides the standardized transport abstraction that enables reliable, deterministic, and auditable communication while remaining independent of specific messaging technologies or transport implementations.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Principles
-+
-+- Messages are immutable.
-+- Messages are schema-driven.
-+- Messaging is transport-independent.
-+- Message delivery is deterministic.
-+- Messaging preserves provenance.
-+- Every message is fully auditable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Scope
-+
-+This specification applies to:
-+
-+- Workflow Services
-+- Parser Services
-+- Validation Services
-+- Repository Services
-+- Mapping Services
-+- Publication Services
-+- Governance Services
-+- Utility Services
-+- Administrative Services
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Messaging Definition
-+
-+Workflow Messaging is the governed exchange of structured messages between services using approved message schemas, routing policies, and delivery guarantees.
-+
-+Messaging SHALL remain independent of any specific implementation technology.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Messaging Objectives
-+
-+Messaging SHALL provide:
-+
-+- Reliable information exchange
-+- Standardized message formats
-+- Deterministic routing
-+- Delivery guarantees
-+- Context propagation
-+- Governance visibility
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Canonical Message Schema
-+
-+Every message SHALL contain:
-+
-+| Field | Required | Description |
-+|--------|----------|-------------|
-+| Message ID | Yes | Globally unique message identifier |
-+| Message Type | Yes | Message classification |
-+| Schema Version | Yes | Message schema version |
-+| Workflow ID | Yes | Associated workflow |
-+| Execution ID | Yes | Workflow execution identifier |
-+| Correlation ID | Yes | Correlation identifier |
-+| Sender Service | Yes | Originating service |
-+| Recipient | Yes | Intended recipient(s) |
-+| Timestamp | Yes | Creation timestamp |
-+| Payload | Yes | Message content |
-+| Metadata | Yes | Operational metadata |
-+| Provenance ID | Yes | Provenance reference |
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Message Identifier
-+
-+Message identifiers SHALL:
-+
-+- Be globally unique.
-+- Never be reused.
-+- Remain immutable.
-+- Support repository indexing.
-+
-+Example:
-+
-+MSG-2026-0000008125
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Message Types
-+
-+Supported message types include:
-+
-+- Command
-+- Response
-+- Event
-+- Notification
-+- Request
-+- Query
-+- Status
-+- Heartbeat
-+- Administrative
-+
-+Additional message types MAY be introduced through governance.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Payload
-+
-+Message payloads SHALL:
-+
-+- Conform to approved schemas.
-+- Be self-describing.
-+- Support version evolution.
-+- Preserve semantic meaning.
-+- Remain immutable after publication.
-+
-+Payload validation SHALL precede transmission.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Metadata
-+
-+Message metadata MAY include:
-+
-+- Priority
-+- Delivery policy
-+- Expiration time
-+- Security classification
-+- Retry count
-+- Compression indicator
-+- Encoding
-+- Tenant identifier
-+
-+Metadata SHALL NOT alter payload semantics.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Message Lifecycle
-+
-+Messages SHALL progress through the following lifecycle:
-+
-+Created
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Validated
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Queued
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Routed
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Delivered
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Acknowledged
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Archived
-+
-+Lifecycle transitions SHALL be recorded.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Message Validation
-+
-+Every message SHALL validate:
-+
-+- Schema compliance
-+- Required fields
-+- Sender identity
-+- Recipient identity
-+- Version compatibility
-+- Payload integrity
-+- Metadata completeness
-+
-+Invalid messages SHALL be rejected.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Delivery Guarantees
-+
-+Messaging SHALL support:
-+
-+- Best Effort
-+- At Least Once
-+- At Most Once
-+- Exactly Once (where supported)
-+
-+The required delivery guarantee SHALL be explicitly declared.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Routing
-+
-+Message routing SHALL determine:
-+
-+- Intended recipients
-+- Delivery priority
-+- Queue selection
-+- Retry handling
-+- Dead-letter handling
-+
-+Routing SHALL remain deterministic.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Message Ordering
-+
-+Where ordering requirements exist, messaging SHALL preserve:
-+
-+- Workflow sequence
-+- Event sequence
-+- Dependency ordering
-+- Causal ordering
-+
-+Ordering requirements SHALL be explicitly declared.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Message Expiration
-+
-+Messages MAY define expiration policies.
-+
-+Expiration SHALL specify:
-+
-+- Expiration timestamp
-+- Expiration behavior
-+- Archive requirements
-+- Notification policy
-+
-+Expired messages SHALL NOT be processed.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Duplicate Detection
-+
-+Messaging SHALL support duplicate detection using:
-+
-+- Message ID
-+- Correlation ID
-+- Delivery history
-+- Configurable deduplication policies
-+
-+Duplicate processing SHALL be prevented whenever required by workflow policy.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Error Handling
-+
-+Messaging SHALL classify errors including:
-+
-+- Validation failure
-+- Routing failure
-+- Delivery timeout
-+- Recipient unavailable
-+- Schema incompatibility
-+- Authorization failure
-+- Transport failure
-+
-+Errors SHALL generate messaging events.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Dead-Letter Handling
-+
-+Messages that cannot be successfully delivered SHALL be directed to governed dead-letter processing.
-+
-+Dead-letter processing SHALL:
-+
-+- Preserve original messages.
-+- Record failure reasons.
-+- Support administrative review.
-+- Enable replay where authorized.
-+
-+Dead-letter repositories SHALL remain auditable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Security
-+
-+Messaging SHALL enforce:
-+
-+- Authentication
-+- Authorization
-+- Message integrity
-+- Confidentiality where required
-+- Replay protection
-+- Audit recording
-+
-+Unauthorized messages SHALL be rejected.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Messaging Events
-+
-+Messaging SHALL publish events including:
-+
-+- Message Created
-+- Message Validated
-+- Message Queued
-+- Message Routed
-+- Message Delivered
-+- Message Acknowledged
-+- Message Rejected
-+- Message Expired
-+- Message Archived
-+
-+Events SHALL conform to the Event Model Specification.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Audit Requirements
-+
-+Messaging SHALL record:
-+
-+- Message ID
-+- Message type
-+- Sender
-+- Recipient
-+- Validation result
-+- Delivery status
-+- Timestamp
-+- Provenance reference
-+
-+Audit records SHALL remain immutable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Monitoring
-+
-+Messaging services SHOULD expose:
-+
-+- Messages created
-+- Messages delivered
-+- Delivery latency
-+- Queue depth
-+- Validation failures
-+- Retry frequency
-+- Dead-letter volume
-+- Delivery success rate
-+
-+Monitoring SHALL support operational governance and capacity planning.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Repository Integration
-+
-+Workflow Service Messaging integrates with:
-+
-+- Workflow Service Communication
-+- Workflow Service Coordination
-+- Workflow Service Discovery
-+- Workflow Service Registry
-+- Workflow Service Dependencies
-+- Workflow Execution
-+- Event Processing
-+- Observability
-+- Repository Indexes
-+- Configuration Management
-+
-+Messaging SHALL preserve repository integrity, deterministic execution, and institutional accountability.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Philosophy
-+
-+Messaging is the standardized exchange mechanism upon which distributed workflow execution depends.
-+
-+By treating messages as immutable, versioned, schema-governed repository assets rather than transient transport artifacts, the Repository Ingestion Framework enables reproducible execution, reliable interoperability, comprehensive observability, and long-term architectural sustainability while remaining independent of specific messaging technologies.
-+
-+---
-+
-+End of Specification.
-\ No newline at end of file
