-+# Workflow Service Resilience Specification
-+
-+**Version:** 0.1  
-+**Status:** Draft  
-+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Purpose
-+
-+The Workflow Service Resilience Specification defines the canonical architecture for ensuring that workflow services continue operating correctly despite failures, resource constraints, dependency disruptions, and unexpected operational conditions.
-+
-+Resilience ensures that repository operations remain reliable, recoverable, deterministic, and auditable while minimizing disruption to workflow execution.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Principles
-+
-+- Resilience is designed, not assumed.
-+- Failures are expected.
-+- Recovery is deterministic.
-+- Fault isolation is mandatory.
-+- State integrity is preserved.
-+- Recovery preserves provenance.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Scope
-+
-+This specification applies to:
-+
-+- Workflow Services
-+- Parser Services
-+- Validation Services
-+- Mapping Services
-+- Repository Services
-+- Event Services
-+- Governance Services
-+- Utility Services
-+- Administrative Services
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Resilience Definition
-+
-+Service Resilience is the ability of a service to continue providing its declared capabilities, or recover them predictably, despite operational failures or adverse conditions.
-+
-+Resilience SHALL preserve repository integrity throughout failure and recovery operations.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Resilience Objectives
-+
-+Service resilience SHALL provide:
-+
-+- Fault tolerance
-+- Controlled degradation
-+- Deterministic recovery
-+- Operational continuity
-+- Dependency isolation
-+- Repository consistency
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Canonical Resilience Schema
-+
-+Every resilience policy SHALL contain:
-+
-+| Field | Required | Description |
-+|--------|----------|-------------|
-+| Resilience Policy ID | Yes | Unique policy identifier |
-+| Service ID | Yes | Associated service |
-+| Policy Version | Yes | Semantic version |
-+| Failure Categories | Yes | Supported failure classifications |
-+| Recovery Strategy | Yes | Defined recovery procedures |
-+| Retry Policy | Yes | Retry behavior |
-+| Timeout Policy | Yes | Timeout configuration |
-+| Circuit Protection | Yes | Circuit management rules |
-+| Escalation Policy | Yes | Operational escalation |
-+| Provenance ID | Yes | Provenance reference |
-+| Metadata | No | Supplemental information |
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Failure Categories
-+
-+Services SHALL classify failures into one or more of the following categories:
-+
-+- Internal Processing Failure
-+- Dependency Failure
-+- Validation Failure
-+- Configuration Failure
-+- Communication Failure
-+- Storage Failure
-+- Resource Exhaustion
-+- Timeout
-+- Security Failure
-+- Administrative Failure
-+
-+Failure categories SHALL be explicitly recorded.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Fault Isolation
-+
-+Services SHALL isolate failures whenever possible.
-+
-+Isolation mechanisms MAY include:
-+
-+- Execution boundaries
-+- Service boundaries
-+- Resource quotas
-+- Independent retry domains
-+- Dependency isolation
-+- Workflow isolation
-+
-+Fault isolation SHALL prevent cascading failures.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Retry Policy
-+
-+Retry policies SHALL define:
-+
-+- Maximum retry attempts
-+- Retry interval
-+- Backoff strategy
-+- Retry conditions
-+- Non-retryable failures
-+
-+Retry behavior SHALL be deterministic and governed through configuration.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Timeout Policy
-+
-+Every service SHALL define timeout policies for:
-+
-+- Initialization
-+- External communication
-+- Repository operations
-+- Dependency invocation
-+- Validation operations
-+- Workflow execution
-+
-+Timeout expiration SHALL trigger defined recovery behavior.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Circuit Protection
-+
-+Services SHOULD implement circuit protection for unstable dependencies.
-+
-+Circuit states SHALL include:
-+
-+- Closed
-+- Open
-+- Half-Open
-+
-+Circuit transitions SHALL be based upon measurable operational evidence.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Graceful Degradation
-+
-+When full functionality cannot be maintained, services SHOULD continue providing approved reduced functionality.
-+
-+Graceful degradation SHALL:
-+
-+- Preserve repository integrity.
-+- Avoid data corruption.
-+- Record degraded behavior.
-+- Maintain auditability.
-+
-+Unsupported degradation SHALL NOT occur.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Recovery Strategies
-+
-+Supported recovery strategies MAY include:
-+
-+- Immediate Retry
-+- Delayed Retry
-+- Checkpoint Restoration
-+- Service Restart
-+- Dependency Re-resolution
-+- Workflow Replay
-+- Administrative Intervention
-+
-+Recovery strategies SHALL be explicitly documented.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# State Preservation
-+
-+During failure handling, services SHALL preserve:
-+
-+- Execution context
-+- Workflow state
-+- Checkpoints
-+- Provenance
-+- Audit history
-+- Correlation identifiers
-+
-+State preservation SHALL enable deterministic recovery.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Dependency Resilience
-+
-+Services SHALL evaluate dependency resilience by verifying:
-+
-+- Availability
-+- Compatibility
-+- Health
-+- Response behavior
-+- Version support
-+
-+Dependency instability SHALL influence recovery decisions.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Resource Protection
-+
-+Services SHOULD protect critical resources including:
-+
-+- Memory
-+- CPU
-+- Storage
-+- Network bandwidth
-+- Repository connections
-+- Event queues
-+
-+Resource exhaustion SHALL trigger governed mitigation strategies.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Escalation
-+
-+Operational escalation SHALL define:
-+
-+- Escalation conditions
-+- Notification targets
-+- Escalation priority
-+- Administrative responsibilities
-+- Resolution tracking
-+
-+Escalation SHALL preserve complete operational history.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Validation
-+
-+Resilience mechanisms SHALL validate:
-+
-+- Recovery success
-+- Repository consistency
-+- Dependency integrity
-+- Workflow continuity
-+- Service health
-+- Event publication
-+
-+Recovery SHALL complete only after successful validation.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Resilience Events
-+
-+Services SHALL publish resilience events including:
-+
-+- Failure Detected
-+- Retry Initiated
-+- Timeout Occurred
-+- Circuit Opened
-+- Circuit Closed
-+- Recovery Started
-+- Recovery Completed
-+- Escalation Triggered
-+- Service Restored
-+
-+Events SHALL conform to the Event Model Specification.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Audit Requirements
-+
-+Resilience operations SHALL record:
-+
-+- Service ID
-+- Failure category
-+- Detection timestamp
-+- Recovery strategy
-+- Retry history
-+- Validation outcome
-+- Responsible component
-+- Provenance reference
-+
-+Audit records SHALL remain immutable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Monitoring
-+
-+Resilience monitoring SHOULD expose:
-+
-+- Failure frequency
-+- Recovery success rate
-+- Retry utilization
-+- Timeout frequency
-+- Circuit breaker status
-+- Dependency failures
-+- Escalation activity
-+- Mean recovery time
-+
-+Monitoring SHALL support operational governance and continuous improvement.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Repository Integration
-+
-+Workflow Service Resilience integrates with:
-+
-+- Workflow Service Health
-+- Workflow Recovery
-+- Workflow Replay
-+- Workflow Checkpointing
-+- Workflow Service Dependencies
-+- Workflow Orchestration
-+- Workflow Execution
-+- Observability
-+- Event Processing
-+- Repository Indexes
-+
-+Resilience management SHALL preserve repository integrity and institutional accountability.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Philosophy
-+
-+Failures are an inevitable aspect of distributed systems and long-running repository workflows.
-+
-+By defining resilience as a governed architectural capability rather than an implementation-specific feature, the Repository Ingestion Framework ensures that services recover predictably, preserve repository integrity, maintain complete auditability, and continue supporting deterministic execution under adverse operational conditions.
-+
-+---
-+
-+End of Specification.
-\ No newline at end of file
