-+# Workflow State Machine Specification
-+
-+**Version:** 0.1
-+**Status:** Draft
-+**Subsystem:** Repository Ingestion Framework
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Purpose
-+
-+The Workflow State Machine Specification defines the canonical execution states and legal state transitions for workflows within the Repository Ingestion Framework.
-+
-+This specification standardizes workflow progression independently of implementation.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Principles
-+
-+- States are explicit.
-+- State transitions are deterministic.
-+- Every transition is recorded.
-+- Invalid transitions are prohibited.
-+- History is immutable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Scope
-+
-+This specification applies to:
-+
-+- Workflow engines
-+- Orchestration services
-+- Workflow definitions
-+- Execution tracking
-+- Monitoring
-+- Recovery services
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Canonical Workflow States
-+
-+Every workflow SHALL exist in exactly one of the following states:
-+
-+- Created
-+- Initialized
-+- Validated
-+- Ready
-+- Running
-+- Waiting
-+- Suspended
-+- Recovering
-+- Completed
-+- Failed
-+- Cancelled
-+- Archived
-+
-+No workflow SHALL exist outside these defined states.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Initial State
-+
-+Every workflow SHALL begin in:
-+
-+Created
-+
-+The Created state indicates that a workflow definition has been instantiated but execution has not yet begun.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Normal Execution Path
-+
-+A successful workflow SHOULD progress through:
-+
-+Created
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Initialized
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Validated
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Ready
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Running
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Completed
-+
-+↓
-+
-+Archived
-+
-+Alternative execution paths MAY include Waiting or Suspended states.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Waiting State
-+
-+A workflow enters Waiting when execution cannot continue because it depends upon:
-+
-+- Another workflow
-+- An external service
-+- Human review
-+- Scheduled execution
-+- Required repository state
-+
-+Waiting workflows SHALL preserve execution context.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Suspended State
-+
-+Suspended workflows have intentionally paused execution.
-+
-+Reasons MAY include:
-+
-+- Administrative action
-+- Resource constraints
-+- Maintenance windows
-+- Operational safety
-+
-+Suspended workflows SHALL remain recoverable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Recovering State
-+
-+Recovering indicates that execution is resuming after interruption.
-+
-+Recovery SHALL preserve:
-+
-+- Workflow ID
-+- Execution ID
-+- Correlation ID
-+- Provenance
-+- Completed work
-+
-+Recovery SHALL resume from the most recent valid checkpoint.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Completed State
-+
-+Completed indicates:
-+
-+- All required steps executed.
-+- Outputs validated.
-+- Required events published.
-+- Execution successfully concluded.
-+
-+Completed workflows become historical records.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Failed State
-+
-+Failed indicates execution terminated unexpectedly.
-+
-+Failure SHALL record:
-+
-+- Failure reason
-+- Responsible component
-+- Timestamp
-+- Diagnostics
-+- Recovery eligibility
-+
-+Failure SHALL preserve completed work.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Cancelled State
-+
-+Cancelled indicates execution terminated intentionally.
-+
-+Cancellation SHALL record:
-+
-+- Initiator
-+- Reason
-+- Timestamp
-+- Completed steps
-+
-+Cancelled workflows SHALL remain searchable.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Archived State
-+
-+Archived workflows are retained solely for institutional history.
-+
-+Archived workflows SHALL:
-+
-+- Remain immutable.
-+- Preserve provenance.
-+- Support auditing.
-+- Support reporting.
-+
-+Archived workflows SHALL NOT execute.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Legal State Transitions
-+
-+The following transitions are permitted:
-+
-+| From | To |
-+|--------|-----|
-+| Created | Initialized |
-+| Initialized | Validated |
-+| Validated | Ready |
-+| Ready | Running |
-+| Running | Waiting |
-+| Waiting | Running |
-+| Running | Suspended |
-+| Suspended | Recovering |
-+| Recovering | Running |
-+| Running | Completed |
-+| Running | Failed |
-+| Running | Cancelled |
-+| Completed | Archived |
-+| Failed | Archived |
-+| Cancelled | Archived |
-+
-+Transitions not listed SHALL be considered invalid.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Transition Rules
-+
-+Every transition SHALL:
-+
-+- Preserve execution history.
-+- Record timestamp.
-+- Record originating state.
-+- Record destination state.
-+- Generate a lifecycle event.
-+
-+Transitions SHALL be atomic.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Validation
-+
-+State transitions SHALL verify:
-+
-+- Current state.
-+- Requested state.
-+- Transition legality.
-+- Workflow integrity.
-+- Dependency satisfaction.
-+
-+Invalid transitions SHALL be rejected.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Monitoring
-+
-+Workflow monitoring SHOULD report:
-+
-+- Current state
-+- Previous state
-+- State duration
-+- Transition count
-+- Recovery count
-+- Failure count
-+
-+Monitoring SHALL support operational analysis.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Repository Integration
-+
-+Workflow state management integrates with:
-+
-+- Workflow Orchestration
-+- Workflow Execution
-+- Event Processing
-+- Lifecycle Management
-+- Observability
-+- Validation
-+- Repository Indexes
-+
-+State transitions SHALL preserve repository consistency.
-+
-+---
-+
-+# Design Philosophy
-+
-+A workflow is not merely a sequence of tasks.
-+
-+It is a governed process whose evolution through time becomes part of the institutional memory of the Repository Ingestion Framework.
-+
-+The state machine provides the constitutional rules that ensure every workflow progresses predictably, transparently, and reproducibly.
-+
-+---
-+
-+End of Specification.
-\ No newline at end of file
